N2  - Cognitive impairment in Parkinson’s disease (PDCI) spans a continuum from mild cognitive impairment (PD-MCI) to dementia (PDD). It represents one of the most disabling non-motor symptoms during disease progression, significantly reducing patients’ quality of life. The underlying pathology mechanisms involve multisystem interactions, including abnormal a-synuclein (a-Syn) aggregation, dopaminergic cholinergic circuit imbalance, tau protein pathology, and neuroinflammation. This review systematically summarizes recent treatment approaches for PD-CI, encompassing pharmacological and non-pharmacological, to providing evidence and novel insights for timely clinical interventions.
