N2  - Background and aims: The development of non-invasive clinical diagnostics is paramount for the early detection of Alzheimer's disease (AD). Neurofibrillary tangles in AD originate from the entorhinal cortex, a cortical memory area that mediates navigation via path integration (PI). Here, we studied correlations between PI errors and levels of a range of AD biomarkers using a 3D virtual reality navigation system to explore PI as a non-invasive surrogate marker for early detection. Methods: We examined 111 healthy adults for PI using a head-mounted 3D VR system, AD-related plasma biomarkers (GFAP, NfL, Aβ40, Aβ42, p-tau181), ApoE genotype, and cognitive assessments, analyzing their covariance to identify predictive relationships for PI errors. Results: We found significant correlations between PI errors with age, plasma GFAP, p-tau181, and NfL. Multivariate analysis identified significant correlations of plasma GFAP (t = 2.16, p = 0.0332) and p-tau181 (t = 2.53, p = 0.0128) with PI errors. Predictor importance ranking using machine learning and receiver operating characteristic curves identified plasma p-tau181 as the most significant predictor of PI. ApoE genotype and plasma p-tau181 showed positive and negative PI associations (ApoE: coefficient = 0.650, p = 0.037; p-tau181: coefficient = −0.899, p = 0.041). EC thickness exhibited negative correlations with age, mean PI errors, and GFAP, NfL, and p-tau181 but these associations did not remain significant in regression analyses. Conclusions: 3D VR-based PI assessment, combined with plasma p-tau181 levels, may serve as a non-invasive biomarker for early AD detection. This combinatorial approach could facilitate timely interventions and improve patient outcomes.
