N2  - Background: Fresh air breaks (FABs) are a well-established strategy to enhance the well-being of psychiatric inpatients by promoting stress reduction, mood stabilization, and sensory relief (Ulrich, 1983). However, access to outdoor spaces is often restricted in locked psychiatric units due to safety concerns, staffing limitations, and logistical barriers. This is particularly challenging for medically complex patients in medical-psychiatric units who may also face mobility issues or cannot be safely transported. Research supports the use of Virtual Reality (VR) as an effective therapeutic tool in psychiatric settings, demonstrating its potential in addressing several psychiatric disorders (Wiebe et al., 2022). The integration of VR in psychiatric inpatient settings has shown promise in improving patient engagement, reducing stress, and offering therapeutic benefits, with studies demonstrating its feasibility and acceptability in high-stress environments (Mark et al., 2021; Rose et al., 2021). In this context, Virtual Fresh Air Breaks (VFABs) emerge as an innovative alternative, offering immersive, nature-based outdoor experiences for patients who are unable to participate in conventional outdoor breaks. This study is the first to evaluate the feasibility, acceptability, and short-term clinical effects of VFABs in a locked inpatient medical-psychiatric unit, aiming to determine whether VFABs can serve as a practical adjunct to traditional FABs. Methods: This study is a prospective, single-session intervention with follow-up assessments. It takes place in a locked inpatient medical-psychiatric unit at Tufts Medical Center. Participants include adult psychiatric inpatients. Exclusion criteria include severe motion sickness, cognitive impairment, or seizure disorders. The intervention consists of a 30-minute VR session simulating calming, nature-based environments. Assessments are conducted pre-VR, immediately post-VR, and at 48 hours using the following validated instruments: Visual Analog Mood and Anxiety Scales, Subjective Units of Distress Scale, System Usability Scale, and the Virtual Reality Sickness Questionnaire. Participants also provide qualitative feedback comparing VR FABs to traditional FABs. A 48-hour post-intervention chart review is conducted to assess for signs of agitation and any adverse effects. Results: Data collection is ongoing, and preliminary results will be shared at ACLP 2025. Discussion: While data collection is still in progress, findings at ACLP 2025 will provide insights into the feasibility, acceptability, and short-term clinical effects of VFABs in a locked inpatient psychiatric unit. It will also provide insight into whether VR-based interventions can be expanded beyond inpatient psychiatry to patients under psychiatric hold on medical floors and those boarding in emergency departments. References: Mark, I.,et al. (2021). The use of virtual reality in a psychiatric intensive care unit: A pilot study. Journal of Psychiatric Intensive Care, 17(2), 123–128. Rose, V., et al. (2021). Bringing the outside in: The feasibility of virtual reality with people with dementia in an inpatient psychiatric care setting. Dementia, 20(1), 106–129. Ulrich, R. S. (1983). Aesthetic and Affective Response to Natural Environment. In I. Altman and J. F. Wohlwill (Eds.), Behavior and the Natural Environment (pp. 85–125). Springer US. Wiebe, A., et al. (2022). Virtual reality in the diagnostic and therapy for mental disorders: A systematic review. Clinical Psychology Review, 98, 102213.
