N2  - Introduction: Social isolation and loneliness are associated with adverse health outcomes in older adults, such as depression and cognitive decline. According to the 2023 U.S. Surgeon General Report, loneliness poses health risks as deadly as smoking up to 15 cigarettes a day. In this context, artificial intelligence (AI) has emerged as a tool that may help to identify social isolation and enhance social connection, providing emotional support, and improving quality of life in older adults. In this review, we critically review available research on how AI-driven technologies have been shown to address loneliness in this population, discuss key barriers to adoption, such as ethical concerns, and discuss further research areas that may help understand the benefits and risks of using AI technologies in this context. Methods: A systematic search was conducted on PubMed in October 2024 using several keywords including “social isolation”, “loneliness”, “artificial intelligence “older adults” and ‘geriatric.” After screening for relevance, 16 articles were identified as directly pertinent to the topic and were included in this review. This included randomized controlled articles, meta-analyses, review articles, viewpoints, and editorials. Results: Identifying Social Isolation and Loneliness: AI based interventions have been proposed as a tool to identify older adults at risk of social isolation and loneliness. For example, AI reviewed electronic health records of patients with prostate cancer to identify individuals at risk of social isolation with 90% precision. Machine learning models predicted loneliness levels with 90-98% accuracy by analyzing mobility patterns collected in a nursing home. It has also been demonstrated that AI can recognize, interpret, and process emotion with the use of vocal biomarkers, facial expressions, eye and body movements, as well as social media behavior monitoring, to detect social isolation and loneliness in this age group. Addressing Social Isolation and Loneliness: A scoping review of 29 studies (18 observational, 11 experimental) found that robots and computer agents positively impacted loneliness, with 69% acting as direct companions, 41% serving as catalysts for social interaction, 10% facilitating remote communication, and 3% reminding users of social engagements. In a randomized controlled trial, interactions with a robot significantly reduced loneliness (p=0.03) compared to standard activities. Another study demonstrated that a robot provided emotional support comparable to living animals for residents in long-term care. Virtual reality platforms have been explored to enable older adults to interact with avatars of loved ones, fostering virtual connections and offering reminiscence therapy through familiar settings. Additionally, home technologies, including sensors, smart devices, and robots, support aging in place while facilitating social connections. Finally, a few studies have investigated AI-based web coaching to help adults with communication difficulties address social isolation and loneliness. Barriers and Concerns: Over-reliance on AI companions may reduce meaningful human interactions, raising ethical concerns about deception and emotional dependency. Data privacy, misinformation risks, and the high cost of technology are significant barriers. Age-related biases about technology adoption further hinder uptake. Personalized interventions are critical, as older adults vary in their acceptance and perception of AI companions. Conclusions: Loneliness among older adults is a public health priority. In recent years, preliminary evidence has suggested that some AI-based interventions may be helpful to identify and combat loneliness and isolation among older adults. However, there have been no head-to-head trials comparing the efficacy of these interventions, and it is possible that some types of AI-based interventions (e.g., robots) are more effective in specific patient populations, such as older adults in long-term care settings. Their effectiveness in patients with cognitive impairment or dementia remains unclear. Further research should focus on personalization, accessibility, and ethical considerations to optimize AI interventions for older adults.
