N2  - Background: Despite of the ongoing debate about what an abnormal amyloid-PET scan clinically means for a person with subjective cognitive decline (SCD), on a group level it is clearly associated with an increased dementia risk. We aimed to gain insight in emotional and information needs of individuals with SCD and their accompanying loved ones (‘care partners’) as expressed during amyloid-PET disclosure consultations, and explore physicians’ communication stimulating these need expressions. Methods: In this observational study, we included 53 participants with SCD (65±7.6yrs, 18(34%)F, MMSE 29±1.4, 14(26%) amyloid-positive) who wished to learn their amyloid-PET results after participating in a study on its predictive value. We audio-recorded the consultations in which amyloid-PET results were shared (54.7% face-to-face, 45.3% telephone). In 10/53 consultations, a care partner was present. All needs expression were identified and categorized, as well as physicians’ communication surrounding these expressions, by two independent coders, partially based on the VR-CoDES. Findings: We observed 1434 expressions of need (n=915 emotional and n=462 information), on average 27 per consultation, with large variation (SD=23). Of these, n=74 were mentioned by a care partner. Individuals with abnormal test-results expressed more needs (t(51)=-3.30, p<0.001). The specific needs that were expressed varied, and could be categorized into 24 themes, for instance including Emotional reaction to results (e.g., relief in case of a normal result), Lifestyle (e.g., the need for lifestyle advice), and Prognosis (e.g., a wish to learn more about the future). Physicians commonly responded by providing space for these expressions (1149/1609; 71.4%), which often led to a continued interaction on individuals’ needs. Discussion: We observed a high frequency and large thematic variety in needs expressed by individuals with SCD during amyloid-PET disclosure consultations. Our findings highlight the importance of tailored communication that is sensitive to these needs and could inform physician and/or patient tools and/or education.
