N2  - Background: Despite modern healthcare advancements, patients with Limited English proficiency (LEP) continue to experience less favorable outcomes in English Speaking healthcare system. Coupled with the anticipated influx of seniors 65 years and older likely to receive care by the year 2030, highly effective and cost-efficient training for clinicians remains a challenge that is ripe for innovative solutions. The authors will share insights from implementing a training model for Resident Physician Anesthesiologists at the University of California, Los Angeles with the intent of improving the standard of equitable and high-quality perioperative care. Methods: Using Standardized Patient actors from our Simulation Center, our Lead Investigator developed three scenarios - Informed Consent, Geriatric Assessment, and Mini-Cog© Early Dementia Detection Screening - focusing on assessing and teaching the unique challenges and strategies of communicating with an interpreter. Additionally, standardized encounters will be captured in 360° video format to explore the feasibility of Virtual Reality as an ad-hoc training experience for future training modalities, including creating virtual simulation cases for trainees to practice before seeing actual patients with limited English proficiency. We will use surveys and standardized checklists to monitor performance indicators before, during and after the scenario for debriefing and qualitative analysis. Findings: We are currently piloting our simulation scenarios and results will be made available at the conference. Discussion: Culturally appropriate communication is fundamental for equitable healthcare access and quality to our vulnerable LEP communities. By tailoring education models to recognize and navigate accessibility flaws, we hope to better equip emerging healthcare providers with the necessary tools to provide a safe and more inclusive patient experience.
