N2  - Introduction: A key symptom of Alzheimer's disease from its earliest stage is spatial impairment. Despite many existing cognitive exercises used for memory improvement, games with spatial cognition focus are lacking. We have developed a new virtual reality navigation game with a focus on strengthening spatial orientation while being enjoyable; the game uses gadgets that are easily accessible, and easy to use even for people with Alzheimer's. These are crucial for the game to be used for rehabilitation in Alzheimer's population. Objectives: To investigate the efficacy of the developed serious game, “Barn Ruins Navigation (BRN)”, as a rehabilitation tool by studying performance on the game over time and assessing pre and post-training performance on an independent spatial assessment. Patients & methods: Four people with mild to moderate Alzheimer's (one female, 74.75±7.85 years) participated in the study so far. They played the BRN game, 30 minutes each day, three times a week for 4 weeks. The game is played on a laptop screen and a gaming controller is used for movement. Performance pre and post-training on a spatial assessment developed and validated by our research group, VRN-House, and the BRN game score are used as the main outcome measures. Results: All participants showed an improvement in the BRN scores. Participants with moderate Alzheimer's started with lower scores on the game but showed a higher rate of improvement than participants with mild Alzheimer's, who perhaps had ceiling effect. The VRN-House assessment showed increased or stable spatial performance at post-training for all participants. Conclusion: Participants verbally commented at various points that they enjoyed playing the game. Everyone's spatial performance in the BRN game improved while maintaining or improving spatial performance in an independent spatial assessment. Thus, the BRN game could potentially be a good spatial orientation training tool for people living with Alzheimer's.
