N2  - Introduction: Alzheimer's disease (AD) in its early stages damages brain regions involved in spatial navigation and route planning, reducing performance in spatial navigation using both egocentric and allocentric strategies. Objectives: We aim to create a behavioral test in a virtual reality (VR) environment to assess navigation performance and compare the performance of participants with preclinical AD to a defined normative aging profile. This research aims to identify specific impairments related to early AD-related neurodegeneration. Methods: We designed a corridor maze in an immersive VR environment. Participants (young healthy adults, older healthy adults, diagnosed with preclinical AD) will navigate this space using a joystick and a VR headset and perform orienting tasks with graded complexity. We will quantify their egocentric and allocentric errors as a function of complexity, parameterized by the length and turns of the route. We will quantify a boundary in the error-complexity space at which the performance drops. This will help us obtain a characteristic profile that we will compare between young and older healthy participants. Once we define the profile for normative aging, we will test participants with preclinical AD to identify specific impairments that may be indicative of early AD-related neurodegeneration. Expected results: Based on previous research, we expect to see that older healthy adults have a better performance (higher error boundaries) in egocentric navigation. On the other hand, preclinical AD participants will show lower performance (lower error boundaries) compared to healthy aged adults in both egocentric and allocentric navigation. Conclusion: Our study introduces a promising and innovative approach to address the critical need for non-invasive, inexpensive, and reliable methods for detecting early-stage Alzheimer's disease. This research may lead to more effective interventions and support for individuals with preclinical AD.
