N2  - Purpose: Dementia is a chronic condition presenting as memory disorders, personality changes, and impaired reasoning. There are 5.6 million Americans affected, a number projected to double by 2060. Among these patients, 20-70% experience dementia-related psychosis (DRP). Patients with DRP experience worse clinical outcomes and impose a greater burden on caregivers due to the hallucination and aggression symptoms present. In 2020, the per capita cost of care for Alzheimers and related dementias (ADRDs) was $278-333 billion. Past treatment options have weak evidence supporting their safety or efficacy. This literature review aims to assess the trends in treatment of DRP. Methods: The 2015 guidelines from The American Psychiatric Association on the use of antipsychotics to treat agitation or psychosis in patients with dementia were reviewed for treatment recommendations. A comprehensive literature review was conducted using PubMed and ClinicalTrials.gov. In PubMed, the following terms were used to generate relevant search results: dementia, Alzheimers disease, agitation, aggression, and treatment. In addition to those search terms, filters were applied to include only full-text clinical trials written in English, published between 2019 and 2024, involving human subjects aged 65 years and older. In ClinicalTrials.gov, searches were performed using the phrase psychosis in dementia and filters were applied to include only phase 3 clinical trials. No specific exclusion criteria were followed. Results from both PubMed and ClinicalTrials. gov were reviewed for relevance and then separated based on the trial status. Results: The 2015 treatment guidelines suggest efficacy for risperidone in treating psychosis and for risperidone, olanzapine, and aripiprazole in treating agitation. PubMed search resulted in 12 phase 3 trials completed with results. Clinical trial.gov searches resulted in 30 phase 3 trials, 14 relevant to the topic, 8 complete, and 4 with results available. Among this data, notable drug candidates include Brexpiprazole, AXS-05, ACP-204, Pimavanserin, and KarXT. Brexpiprazole and AXS-05 looked specifically at agitation. Drug candidates ACP-204 and KarXT are currently recruiting candidates for phase 3 trials for psychosis-associated symptoms. Brexpiprazole is a partial D2 and 5-HT1A agonist, and 5-HT2A antagonist. AXS-05 (dextromethorphan/bupropion) is an NMDA receptor antagonist. ACP-204 and Pimavanserin both have agonistic effects at the 5-HT2A receptor. KarXT (xanomeline-trospium) is a muscarinic antipsychotic that acts as a dual M1/M4 muscarinic acetylcholine receptor agonist in the CNS. Brexpiprazole is the first and only drug approved for the treatment of agitation associated with dementia due to Alzheimers disease. Pimavanserin has a phase 3 trial that stopped early for efficacy in time from randomization to relapse. AXS-05 has some promising agitation-related efficacy results while ACP-204 and KarXT are pending results. Conclusion: Dementia-related psychosis is a disease rising in prevalence, especially with the increase in the aging population. Furthermore, the burden on patients, caregivers, and society is projected to increase. Currently, multiple drug candidates for use in DRP have been identified. Brexpiprazole is the first and only approved medication for agitation associated with dementia. Medications currently in late-phase development include pimavanserin, ACP-204, and KarXT to name a few. Non-pharmacologic interventions such as virtual reality have also demonstrated evidence for their use in managing DRP. Overall, there appears to be a promising trend in the drug development space for this burdensome condition.
