N2  - Purpose: Alzheimer's Disease (AD) and dementia affect millions of people globally, impairing memory, cognition, and behavior, leading to reduced quality of life (QoL). While pharmacologic treatments aim to slow disease progression and manage symptoms, nonpharmacologic interventions focus on enhancing QoL, which address behavioral and psychological symptoms. Nonpharmacologic interventions, including Augmented or Virtual Reality (AR/VR), are emerging tools for managing cognitive, behavioral, and emotional symptoms associated with these conditions. This study evaluates current literature on the use of AR/VR technologies in managing cognitive function, mood, daily living activities, physical fitness, and social engagement related to Alzheimer's Disease and dementia. Methods: A comprehensive systematic literature review was carried out by searching PubMed and Google Scholar to identify and evaluate studies published between 2019 and 2023. Search terms used included augmented reality or AR, virtual reality or VR, Alzheimer's disease, and dementia. VR/AR interventions included exercise and cognitive training programs and using wearable headset-based technology systems. Studies that assessed the impact of AR and VR technologies on mild cognitive impairment were included, along with those that assessed a broad range of outcomes, including cognitive performance, physical health, emotional well-being, and functional abilities in activities of daily living (ADLs). By incorporating multiple outcome measures, this review aimed to provide a comprehensive understanding of the potential benefits and limitations of AR and VR interventions in this patient population of Alzheimer's Disease and dementia patients. Results: Seven clinical studies were identified and reviewed from a PubMed and Google Scholar search, including a total of almost 400 patients throughout. Outcomes were measured from survey-based and qualitative interview responses. One study by Matsangidou et al. finds significant reductions in the negative emotions of fear and anxiety after 15-minute VR headset exposures. Similarly, Feng et al. demonstrated significant emotional state improvement with higher interactivity and engagement when using an AR social robot system in dementia patients. Baldimtsi et al. identified statistically significant improvements in global cognitive function and maintenance in memory function in a 12-week intervention of VR-training compared to their physical exercise group. AR interventions, particularly those supporting ADLs, showed functional independence and cognitive performance. Rohrbach et al. established no clear positive effects in completing ADLs, but patients displayed increased time and fewer sequencing errors when completing tasks. Despite the positive findings, limitations such as cost, comfort, disorientation, and distress were noted, especially in patients with moderate to severe cognitive impairments. Future research should address optimal intervention durations, frequencies, and settings, as well as the tools used to assess the long-term impact of AR/VR in cognitive dysfunction. Conclusion: Findings support the growing potential of AR/VR as nonpharmacologic models for the assistance in managing cognitive impairment shown in early Alzheimer's or dementia patients. AR systems provide cognitively impaired patients with assistance to function independently and comprehend directions, improving concentration and attention. These technologies offer potential benefits in enhancing cognitive function, emotional well-being, physical abilities, and social engagement. A foundation for potential long-term management of cognitive decline through consistent training using available VR and AR systems can be established. However, further large-scale studies are required to determine this long-term efficacy and integration of AR and VR into clinical practice.
