N2  - To reveal the progression patterns, early diagnosis, and intervention of Alzheimers disease (AD) pathology in the aging population of the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area, the Greater-Bay-Area Healthy Aging Brain Study (GHABS) aging cohort (ClinicalTrials Registration Number: NCTO6183658) was initiated following the standard protocol of AD cohort in 2021. This study aims to address the major scientifc challenges related to AD by combining high-tech methods in cognitive assessments, digital biomarkers, biochemical techniques, and multimodal neuroimaging to reveal the progression patterns, risk factors, and protective indicators of AD pathology in the older Chinese population. Over the past few years, we investigated the diagnostic performance of PET and MRI imaging, CSF and plasma fuid markers, and digital cognitive test markers in detecting AD-related early abnormal changes. We determined the brainstem as an optimal reference region for calculating Aβ SUVR for a new Aβ tracer 18F-D3FSP to quantify cortical Aβ plaques in the brain. Besides, we made head-to-head comparisons of diagnostic performance among plasma p-tau181, p-tau231, p-tau217, brain-derived tau, and N-terminal tau among 1085 older adults. We demonstrated the comprehensive superiority of plasma p-tau217 in detecting multiple aspects of AD-related pathological changes and tracking dynamically rapid disease progression. Recently, we evaluated the performance of an artifcial intelligence (AI) -assisted virtual reality (VR) digital cognitive test in detecting AD-related pathological changes and cognitive decline in Chinese older adults. Our results suggest that the AI-VR digital cognitive tool may be a promising biomarker for detecting AD pathological alteration and impaired cognition, providing novel digital technologies for early AD screening and diagnosis. These findings help support early AD diagnosis in older adults, particularly for the East Asian Aging population.
