N2  - Introduction: Psychosocial interventions are essential for people with dementia to reduce and/or compensate the impact of cognitive impairment on their daily lives. In 2021, the Fondation Médéric Alzheimer published the guide “Psychosocial interventions and dementia: Understanding, knowing, implementing” dedicated to professionals and presenting theoretical and practical specificities of 10 interventions. Given the success and to meet demand, a new edition was published. Method: This new version was developed in two stages: An update of initial content and a call for contribution to provide new information. Results: The initial following chapters were updated: Adapted physical activity, animal assisted interventions, art therapy, horticultural therapy, cognitive rehabilitation, cognitive stimulation therapy, dance-based interventions, multisensory stimulation, music therapy, and reminiscence therapy. The summary tables indicating which interventions have effects on which symptoms have been updated considering the scientific literature between 2021 and 2023. The new content was selected by a jury of professionals and academics from 29 submissions and includes focus sections about specific interventions and devices: Theatre-mediated interventions, tango therapy, Music Care, the robot PARO. A section is dedicated to digital devices, as it is essential to understand what they are, how they are used and what they can provide for people with dementia. Three types of digital device are presented: Virtual reality, augmented reality and animaloid social robots. Conclusion: This guide is the result of collaborative work between professionals and academics. It is not a substitute for training required to provide these interventions but provides theoretical and practical knowledge to better implement them.
