N2  - Background Subtle cognitive changes are described in premanifest Huntington Disease expansion gene carriers (preHD) including social cognition deficits. However, it is unknown if social cognition is part of the individual history or emerge as a marker of overt disease. Social cognition is not easy to assess, and the type of task can also impact results. Aims We aimed to investigate social cognition long before the theoretical onset of the disease and to determine the effect of the task used. Methods Seven participants with preHD (CAG expansion ≥ 39 repeats; UHDRS <5; age: 35.3 ± 4.9 years) far away from the theoretical onset (CAPS ≤ 0.67 [1]) and 4 patients with manifest HD (HD; age: 59.3 ± 4.6) were included. Participants underwent cognitive evaluation including Stroop test, assessment of social cognition with pencil paper tasks (Mini- SEA) and the REALSoCog task [2], a novel naturalistic and multidimensional task displayed in a non-immersive virtual environment. Performance was compared to normative data. Results All HD patients showed severe slowing and social cognition disorders regardless the task used. In contrast, only 43% of preHD individuals showed impaired Mini-SEA score and lower performance on the REALSoCog task while Stroop performance was intact. Interestingly, the remaining preHD invididuals with normal Stroop and normal Mini-SEA score showed lower performance on the REALSoCog task. Conclusions This study confirms that social cognition impairment is present at premanifest stages, even a long time before theoretical onset. The use of naturalistic tasks is more sensitive to detect those deficits.
