N2  - Introduction: It is well known that circadian rhythms are integral for sleep regulation, but the impact of circadian timing on sleep is less understood. This question is relevant to aging adults at risk for neurodegenerative diseases, as research demonstrates sleep/circadian disturbances in Alzheimer's disease. This study investigates the relationship between circadian rhythms and sleep in older adults at risk of cognitive decline, as well as the impacts of age, sex, and the APOE4 Alzheimer's risk gene. Method: This research utilizes data from the Lifestyle Enriching Activities for Research in Neuroscience Intervention Trial. Sixtyeight older adults ages 55-80 experiencing early cognitive changes wore a GENEActiv accelerometer for 30 days. Accelerometer data was processed using the R packages GGIR, nparACT, and ActCR. Circadian rhythm measures include interdaily stability (IS) (rhythm synchronization), intradaily variability (IV) (fragmentation), and Up- MESOR/Down-MESOR, which signify timing of activity increasing 242 of 633 ABSTRACTS above or below mean activity level, respectively, with lower values indicative of earlier circadian phase. Sleep is quantified by sleep efficiency. Linear regression models were conducted between circadian rhythms and sleep, and were adjusted for age, sex, and APOE4 status. Significant associations were tested for interactions with covariates. Results: This trial consists of 68 older adults (63.2% female, 26.5% APOE4 carriers). IS (b = 0.28, p = 0.00018) and IV (b = 0.094, p = 0.013) were associated with sleep. Interestingly, there was a positive relationship between Up-MESOR and sleep (b = 0.0090, p = 0.016), but a negative relationship between Down-MESOR and sleep (b = 0.010, p = 0.0044), suggesting that a later rise in activity but an earlier decline promotes better sleep. These novel insights inform on how the duration of activity rising and falling influences sleep. As such, mean activity period length (MAPL) was calculated by subtracting Up-MESOR from Down-MESOR. Indeed, there was a significant association between MAPL and sleep efficiency (b = 0.0076, p = 0.038), indicating that shorter activity durations promote better sleep. There was a significant interaction with sex in the relationship between Down-MESOR and sleep (b = 0.013, p = 0.047), and with APOE4 in the relationship between IS and sleep (b = 0.31, p = 0.045). Conclusion: More robust circadian rhythms and shorter activity duration are associated with better sleep. These relationships are impacted by age, sex, and APOE4 status. Conflict of Interest: Yes- Funding disclosure-My research is funded by the National Institute of Aging (NIA) Research Supplements to Promote Diversity in Health-Related Research (PA-21-071) under the NIA parent grant Targeted Physical and Cognitive Activity in a VR Environment in Older Adults at Risk for Alzheimer's Disease (project period: 03/2020-02/2026) led by Dr. Judy Pa at the University of California, San Diego.
