N2  - Path integration, the continuous updating of positional information based on sensory and body-based cues, plays a vital role in our daily lives. Grid cells have been found to be causally involved in path integration. However, in aged humans and individuals at genetic risk for Alzheimer's disease, there is evidence of a decrease in the peak of activity and temporal stability of grid-like BOLD representations over time [1-2]. The underlying cause of these perturbations in the grid code remains unclear. Computational models propose that the speed of movement and heading direction are key inputs to the grid system [3]. In support of this hypothesis, recent research has discovered speed-sensitive and head-direction cells in the medial entorhinal cortex (MEC). In a mouse model of tauopathy, impairments were observed in both grid-cell activity and velocity coding, while head-direction coding in the MEC remained intact [4]. Building upon these findings, we hypothesize that the extent to which the grid-like BOLD signal is modulated by motion speed may predict its integrity. To test this hypothesis, 29 healthy young participants underwent fMRI scanning while performing two virtual reality navigation tasks. The first task involved a linear-Track path integration task, where participants traversed different distances at various speeds. At the end of each trial, they estimated the traveled distance using a visual analog scale. The second task was a passive navigation task in a virtual room to measure the strength and temporal stability of grid-like representations. Both univariate and multivariate analysis methods are used to examine the relationship between speed sensitivity and the stability of the grid code. Furthermore, these methods are applied to investigate whether traversed distances could be extracted from the collective activity of voxels displaying grid-like activity. Finally, the study aims to determine whether the neural modulation by speed could predict behavioral performance in distance estimation. The data collection phase has been completed, and preliminary behavioral analysis revealed a regression to the mean, where longer distances were underestimated and shorter distances were overestimated. The comparison of estimation errors between runs indicated no learning or exhaustion effect. Preliminary univariate analysis revealed different regions to be sensitive to changes in speed, including the motion sensitive hMT+ complex.
