N2  - Background/aims: Despite chronic illness being a major risk factor for negative psychological outcomes, psychological wellbeing is commonly overlooked in palliative care. An innovative treatment that may reduce negative psychological sequelae and ameliorate quality of life, in people with chronic physical conditions, is immersive virtual reality (VR). The aim of this study was to systematically review literature assessing use of immersive VR in adult chronic physical illness populations to: 1. Explore the psychotherapeutic applications of immersive VR in adults with chronic physical illness 2. Assess the effect of immersive VR interventions on the psychological welfare of adults with chronic physical illness. Methods: A systematic literature review and meta-analysis were conducted. Database searches of Ovid MEDLINE/PubMed, Web of Science, PsycINFO, Embase and Scopus were inclusive of literature published between July 1993 to March 2023. Results: From the 12811 texts screened; 31 studies were included. From these studies, 75% of participants were female, and the median age of participants was 50.8 years. Immersive VR interventions promoting relaxation and participant distraction through engagement were shown to have high acceptability and feasibility among patient populations with cancer, kidney disease, dementia, cardiovascular disease, and multiple sclerosis. Many of the texts included were feasibility or pilot studies. The paucity and heterogeneity of study design and interventions made analysis of literature difficult. However, findings suggest that especially in cancer population groups, immersive VR can produce beneficial effects on symptoms of anticipatory anxiety, and pain relief. Conclusions: Immersive VR involving environment-based and gamebased relaxation offer innovative techniques with positive effects amongst people with cancer and potentially in other debilitating longterm physical ailments. Further research is required focussing on the long-term benefits of VR.
