N2  - Background/aims: Chronic physical illness poses an elevated risk for adverse psychological outcomes. Immersive virtual reality (VR) has emerged as a therapeutic modality capable of mitigating these adverse effects and improving quality of life for people with chronic physical illness. This study aimed to systematically review literature examining immersive VR use in adult chronic physical illness populations to understand: 1) How is immersive VR utilised to improve psychological wellbeing of adults with chronic physical illness? 2) What effect does this immersive VR have on the psychological wellbeing of adults with chronic physical illness? Methods: Systematic literature review and meta-analysis. Extensive searches encompassing MEDLINE/PubMed, PsycINFO, Embase, Web of Science, and Scopus databases, spanning from July 1993 to March 2023, were undertaken. Studies were screened for eligibility and data were extracted and appraised according to Cochrane Collaboration guidelines. Meta-analyses were undertaken where possible alongside a narrative report of findings. Results: Out of a pool of 12,811 identified texts, 31 studies met the inclusion criteria. Immersive VR interventions categorized as Relaxing and Engaging, were both acceptable and feasible among adults with conditions such as cancer, dementia, cardiovascular disease, kidney disease, and multiple sclerosis. Notably, a significant portion of the reviewed studies were feasibility or pilot studies, with limited information on efficacy. The available evidence, primarily pertaining to individuals with cancer, suggests that immersive VR interventions can yield positive outcomes in terms of alleviating anticipatory anxiety symptoms and pain. Conclusions: The findings of this study indicate that environment-based and game-based immersive VR interventions present innovative approaches with beneficial effects for individuals with cancer and the potential for similar advantages in the context of other chronic physical illnesses.
