N2  - Background and aims: Our team previously designed and tested a “Peer Mentor Support and Caregiver Education” (PERSEVERE) program, initially in Parkinson's Disease, then Lewy Body Dementia (LBD) given high LBD family caregiver strain. PERSEVERE paired LBD family caregivers together as mentor-mentee dyads for 16 weeks, discussing a modular curriculum during weekly calls, with improved LBD knowledge, attitudes, and 100% program satisfaction. In preparation for randomized controlled testing and in response to feedback, we aimed to [1] revise and test the acceptability of a 12-week curriculum; [2] design and assess usability of a digitized curriculum platform; and [3] determine acceptability of virtual reality (VR) modules for caregiver education. Methods: We applied community-based participatory research (CBPR) and user-centered design to refine PERSEVERE. Participants engaged in three tasks: [1] focus group following individual handbook review, [2] exploration and rating of study website using the validated System Usability Scale (SUS), and [3] focus group following VR module viewing. We interpreted feedback via framework analysis. Results: 21 caregivers (11% male, 10% non-White) provided positive comments overall, emphasizing PERSEVERE's value and applicability. Based on feedback, we reorganized handbook topics and added prompts on cultivating patience, humor, and joy to promote self-care. SUS ratings indicated high ease of website usage (mean 84.4/100, SD 14.7). Participants deemed VR vignettes “vital to see”, advocating for incorporation into the curriculum. Conclusions: Using CBPR and user-centered design, our team refined, digitized, and supplemented resources to optimize and prime for national testing a peer mentor-led multimodal educational intervention for LBD caregivers.
