N2  - Background and aims: The Brain Health Support Program (BHSP) of the CAN-Thumbs Up Study is an online, interactive, educational intervention designed to increase dementia literacy, self-efficacy and address modifiable lifestyle risk factors. Recruiting for a virtual study presented both opportunities and challenges. This included the need to attract participants beyond typical referrals from specialty clinics, and to reach a geographically diverse Canadian population less feasible with in-person evaluations. Methods: A comprehensive recruitment strategy for a virtual environment was implemented. A central website provided any interested candidates with study information, and a secure link to register and provide basic demographic information. Potential participants were recruited through CCNA specialty clinics or existing cohorts, through geotargeting mailing to specific target populations throughout Canada, social medial advertising, partner organizations, and a coordinated National press release. Results: A total of 964 participants agreed to be contacted. Of these, 18–81 were enrolled per month. Of 7569 geotargeted postcards, 153 individuals agreed to be contacted (2%). Social media advertising generated 974 clicks, with 6.8% agreeing to be contacted. The majority of study interest came from earned media and word of mouth. Recruitment was geographically diverse, 45% urban, 35% suburban, and 20% rural. Conclusions: Recruitment for the BHSP demonstrates the value of relatively underutilized recruitment strategies, including earned media, geotargeted mailing, and social media advertising. We also show the value of a centralized website for initial participant screening. These recruitment approaches can be further scaled up, and will likely play a major role in future recruitment for the broader CAN-Thumbs Up Study.
