N2  - Background & Purpose To determine safety and efficacy of using REAL VR as part of acute stroke rehabilitation. Methods We performed a retrospective, proof-of-concept study looking at 10 patients at a tertiary care center over the age of 18 and admitted to hospital services from 7/1/2021 to 1/1/2022 with hemorrhagic or ischemic stroke, an NIH < 6, upper extremity motor deficit < Ys, and requiring truncal support. Patients were excluded if they had dementia, expressive aphasia, delirium, inability to participate in physical therapy or utilize the virtual reality hardware. Enrolled patients received treatment with REAL virtual reality system up to 3 times as part of standard-of-care. Demographics, functional scoring, complications and adverse events were collected during the study. Results Results Thirteen patients initiated VR REAL rehabilitation program at WMC NeurolCU and step-down unit from 7/1/2020-1/1/2021, and ten patients continued until completion. The mean age of patients who completed the program was 73.6, and the cohort that were male was 50%. Half of the cohort were admitted for ischemic stroke. Other reasons for admission included hemorrhage stroke (30%) and transient ischemic attack (20%). The median admissio|i-to-rehabilitation time was 4 days (IQR 3-5). Mean length of stay, 11.1 days. Majority of cohort was discharged to outpatient rehabilitation (80%) and 20% of the cohort were discharged home. The median number of sessions was one. Four patients utilized REAL VR program more than once. Average duration of rehabilitation session was 3.66 minutes. No adverse events were reported during use of the REAL rehabilitation program. However, two patients (15.4%) dropped out during the program, commenting that the program was "childish", or "not fun". Conclusion Our study demonstrated the safety of virtual reality in acute strokes. Our results showed VR therapy, in the form of REAL, is a safe adjunct therapy for stroke rehabilitation, and can become a new method of post-stroke intervention.
