N2  - Background: We have developed a reminiscence program for people living with dementia to improve their autobiographical memory and reduce neuropsychiatric symptoms. In this paper, the objective is to present findings from a pilot's virtual reality experience with elderly people without dementia. Method: 4 people over 60 years of age from a non-governmental organization in Mexico City that carry out active and healthy aging activities were invited to participate, the inclusion criteria: people without dementia or other mental health problems and who accepted participate voluntarily. A focus group was held to find out their perceptions about the use of virtual reality as a therapeutic tool. Afterwards, the virtual reality glasses were placed on each of the participants, with a duration of 10 minutes. The content of the video was related to images of childhood in a context of the 40's and 50's. At the end, the participants met again and commented on their experience. The conduct of the focus groups and the feedback was in charge of a psychologist, and accompanied by a staff of 2 engineers and three co-investigators, for the care of the participants. Result: None of the 4 participants had used virtual reality, one of them had only heard her grandchildren talk about it. They thought that technology can help in everyday life, but they were unaware that virtual reality could serve as a therapeutic tool to support people living with dementia. After using the virtual reality glasses, one of the participants remembered a brother who had died at the age of 5 years and that it was something she had never talked about, she imagined that it was really her brother and some tears flowed. Another participant commented that she had remembered her family when she was a child. In general, they commented that the experience was pleasant and they would like to continue using virtual reality. Conclusion: In this first approach in older people without dementia, the acceptance of the use of technology in a therapeutic environment and accompanied by mental health professionals to provide support and feedback about the content is shown.
