N2  - Background: Early diagnosis of mild cognitive impairments (MCI) is important for the adequate management of dementia. The manifestation of MCI includes not only cognitive but also motor, sensory and behavioral domains. Screening tools for cognitive impairments show limitations because of being conducted primerily using pen-and-paper and in test laboratory conditions. Performance of screening tests using augmented reality (AR) and virtual reality (VR) technologies can facilitate data processing, providing multisensory interaction of subject with real environment. Screening tests for early detection of cognitive declines implementing smart technology solutions, need to be examined to prove their validity and usability. Objective: The present study tests a protocol for implementation and usability of AR/VR-enhanced multimodal screening tool for early detection of cognitive impairments in the elderly. Methods: In this cross-over design study 100 healthy participants (aged 60-75 years) are screened for cognitive declines using validated tests: Dual-Task Assessment, Trail-Making Test, Reaction Time Test and Erlangen Test of Activities of Daily Living in Persons with Mild Dementia or Mild Cognitive Impairment. The tests are preformed once using conventional software or penand- paper (block 1) and once-implementing tailor-made software and AR/VR hardware (block 2). The 2 blocks are carried out in randomized order. The usability of the AR/VR-enhanced assessments is tested on 10 investigators, 10 test participants and on 2 usability experts using the co-discovery and the cognitive walkthrough Method. Results: This research was funded by the Austrian Research Promotion Agency, grant N 866873. The AR/VR prototype and the study protocol were developed. The study will be completed by August 2022 and the Results will be reported at the 12th World Congress of Neurorehabilitation.
