N2  - Striatal-enriched protein tyrosine phosphatase (STEP) is exclusively expressed in the central nervous system and regulates various neuronal signaling factors through the dephosphorylation of different substrates. Dysregulated expression or uncontrollable en-zymatic activity of STEP contributes to neurological disorders such as Alzheimer's dis-ease, which makes it a promising pharmaceutical target. Herein, we have reviewed the structure and biological functions of STEP, as well as the recent development of small-molecule STEP modulators. We hope this review will provide a reference for the further development of more potent and selective STEP inhibitors for the treatment of nervous system diseases.
