N2  - Research Objectives: Cognitive workload refers to the amount of mental effort allocated to perform a task. Cognitive workload can either be assessed through subjective self-report (e.g., NASA Task Load Index, NASA-TLX) or objective neurophysiological measures (e.g., pupillary response). The aim of this pilot study was to investigate the relationship between subjective and objective measures of cognitive workload in older adults with and without preclinical Alzheimer’s Disease (AD). Design: Cross-sectional, observational study. Setting: University of Kansas Medical Center. Participants: Classification of preclinical AD was based on the presence of β amyloid using amyloid PET scanning. The preclinical AD group (amyloid elevated) included 14 participants [age: 74 (±5), sex: 5 females, Montreal Cognitive Assessment; MOCA:28 (±1.4)]; while those without preclinical AD (amyloid non-elevated) included 7 participants [age: 73.3 (±5), sex: 11 females, MOCA: 27 (±2.3)]. Interventions: Not applicable. Main Outcome Measures: Cognitive workload was assessed during a cognitively demanding car following task in a driving simulator. Self-reported, subjective NASA-TLX scores obtained after test completion were compared to pupillary responses obtained in real-time during the car following event. Non-parametric Spearman correlation tests were used. Results: NASA-TLX showed strong positive correlation [r=0.75, p=0.05] with pupillary response in individuals without preclinical AD. In pre-clinical AD, NASA-TLX scores indicated inverse weak correlation [r=-0.27, p=0.37] with pupillary response. Conclusions: Self-reported cognitive workload correlates strongly with pupillary response in participants without pre-clinical AD, but not in individuals with preclinical AD. More research is warranted to determine the relationship between objective and subjective measures of cognitive workload in individuals at risk of cognitive impairments. Author(s) Disclosures: No authors have conflicts of interest. Keywords: Cognition, Alzheimer, Older, Driving, Virtual Reality
