N2  - 1 Introduction: Due to dramatically increasing deaths from dementia and growing elderly population at the risk of developing dementia in the next decades, early diagnosis of dementia becomes more important [1,2]. To screen the large population at risk, saccadic eye movement could be a promising simple biomarker for the early detection [3]. We integrated virtual reality (VR) technology into saccade assessment, assuming that VR could enhance the potential of evaluation system [4]. We aimed at assessing the validity of our construct with the new saccade test system in healthy older adults, with the hypothesis that complicated VR environments would influence saccadic eye movement. 2 Research question: Can we observe the differences in saccadic eye movement between different VR environments? 3 Methods: 10 healthy elderly aged at 72.0 ± 4.0 years were recruited and screened using the Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA). The assessment system consisted of stabilometer (GP-5000 by ANIMA, Japan) and VR headset (VIVE Pro Eye by HTC, Taiwan). We measured postural sway and ocular movement simultaneously in 8 conditions: pro- and anti-saccade tasks in 4 VR environments: (a) simple 2D (baseline), (b) complicated 3D, (c) 2D with visuospatial memory (VM) load, and (d) 2D with spatial orientation (SO) load. The participants were wearing the VR headset while standing on the stabilometer. In (a) and (b), the participants moved eyes toward a target on either of left and rights sides (pro-saccade) or the opposite direction to the target (anti-saccade). In (c), the participants remembered two shapes and then moved eyes toward the shape that appeared shortly before (pro-saccade) or the shape that did not (anti-saccade). In (d), the participants understood the position of circle target on bird’s-eye view and then moved eyes toward a corresponding circle (pro-saccade) or the opposite circle (anti-saccade) on egocentric view. We calculated latency, peak velocity, and error rate of saccades and performed non-parametric ANOVA to compare these oculo-metrics between test conditions. 4 Results: The participants scored MoCA points (27.8 ± 1.6), achieving cut-off score proposed in [5]. Significant differences were observed between VR scenery in latency and error rate (P < 0.001) and between saccade tasks in all metrics (P < 0.011). 5 Discussion: The results imply that latency and error rate of saccade tasks would be useful measurement parameters in evaluating the effect of different VR environments. SO task might be the most difficult probably because the participants had more difficulty in switching allocentric map to egocentric map than in performing the other tasks, causing longer latency and higher error rate. Nonetheless, VM task also affected these oculo-metrics significantly probably due to the dual-task load: recover short-term memory and perform saccades. 3D environment intentionally affecting stereopsis, visual attention, contrast sensitivity, and colour perception caused longer mean latency (P = 0.016) and more but less significant number of errors (P = 0.6).
