N2  - Background: Over half of all dementia caregivers provide care without training. Current medical graduates report feeling unprepared to counsel patients with dementia. Effective dementia education programs for family caregivers and healthcare providers can lead to improved care practices and patient outcomes. The Dementia Immersion Simulation Experience (DISE) is a multicomponent educational program aimed at increasing learners' knowledge of dementia, eliciting empathy, and improving communication skills with people living with dementia. The study aimed to evaluate DISE's effectiveness in a group of interdisciplinary healthcare providers, family caregivers, and paid caregivers. Methods: 2-hour program entailed virtual reality, simulation experience, videos and a didactic session. A dementia knowledge questionnaire was administered before and after the program. Participants completed a program evaluation including open-ended questions. Results: 130 participants completed the pre and post dementia knowledge questionnaire. 29.2% were family caregivers, 19.2% were paid caregiver, and 51.6% were healthcare professionals or trainees. After the program, the mean score of all participants was higher (p<0.0001) and increased the total number of well-informed participants (p<0.0001) except for the paid caregivers, all of which started with a knowledge gap and none were converted to well-informed. Family caregivers had the highest mean improvement scores (p<0.0002). Content analysis of responses revealed that the program elicited empathy and knowledge of dementia in the majority of participants. Participants also rated the DISE program on a scale of 1 (Strongly Disagree) to 5 (Strongly Agree) across ten categories, covering objectives, relevance, effectiveness, and value of the learning experience. At least 95% of respondents agreed or better (score = 4 or 5) with each evaluation statement and at least 75% strongly agreed with each statement. Conclusions: The mean increase in participant scores and program evaluation ratings suggest the effectiveness of the DISE program in increasing knowledge of dementia. Themes gathered from content analysis of responses to open-ended questions further validated that across various groups, DISE is effective at increasing empathy and skills to communicate with people living with dementia. .
