N2  - The potential of virtual reality (VR) in smart health and health care become more urgent as not just a smart addition to the suite of services but a necessary one as well. VR in smart health is playing an increasingly larger role to improve health outcomes. The true potential of VR in health care has emerged in a variety of medical specialties such as robotic surgery, diagnosis of some difficult diseases, and relieve burn pains, etc. One of the difficult diseases to diagnose is Dementia. VR technique plays a vital role as a decision support system in the diseases diagnostic test and allows the patients to immerse in a controlled environment. The study suggested a non-invasive, cognitive computerized test based on 3D virtual environments in order to detect memory loss, visuospatial defects and spatial navigation. The experimental was applied on 105 real patients, thirty of those had a dementia, sixty-five were cognitively healthy and ten of those had a mild cognitive impairment (MCI). The performance of VR system was compared with Mini-Cog test where the latter is used to measure cognitive impaired patients in the traditional diagnosis system at the clinic. In conclusion, visuospatial and memory recall scores in both clinical diagnosis and VR system of dementia patients were less than those of MCI patients, and the scores of MCI patients were less than those of the control group. Furthermore, there is a perfect agreement between the standard methods in functional evaluation and navigational ability in our system where P-value in weighted Kappa statistic= 100%; and between Mini-Cog-clinical diagnosis vs VR scores where P-value in weighted Kappa statistic= 93%.
