N2  - INTRODUCTION: Aberrant dopamine circuit activity contributes to a variety of neuropsychiatric illnesses including movement disorders, dementia, addiction, obesity, psychosis, and mood disorders. A first step towards developing circuit-based interventions for these conditions is to better understand the information encoded by dopamine neurons. Rapid phasic activity of midbrain dopamine neurons have been conventionally thought to encode reward prediction errors (RPEs). However, recent studies have demonstrated that dopamine signals also slowly increase as a reward is approached. These ramping dopamine signals have been thought to represent state values rather than RPEs. Distinguishing between RPE and value has been challenging in conventional behavioral assays due to the coupled nature of experimental variables. METHODS: We developed novel experimental paradigms that disambiguate RPEs from values using experimental mice in visual virtual reality. We examined dopamine circuit activity in the brains of awake behaving mice at various levels. We measured somatic spiking via single unit electrophysiology of optogenetically identified dopaminergic neurons. We measured axonal calcium signals via fiber photometry of genetically encoded calcium sensors. Finally, we measured striatal dopamine concentrations via fiber photometry of genetically encoded dopamine sensors. RESULTS: We demonstrate that ramping dopamine signals are observed at all stages examined, including somatic spiking, axonal calcium signals, and striatal dopamine concentrations. We further demonstrate that these signals are consistent with RPEs rather than value. CONCLUSION: We demonstrate that ramping dopamine signals in the brain are consistent with RPEs rather than value. We provide a unified computational understanding of rapid phasic and slowly ramping dopamine signals: dopamine neurons perform a derivative-like computation over values on a moment-by-moment basis. Improved understanding of the information encoded by dopamine neurons may facilitate the development of circuit-based interventions for neuropsychiatric illnesses.
