N2  - Background: Cognitive training may have therapeutic benefit in patients with Mild cognitive impairment (MCI). This pilot study examined long-term electrophysiological effects of cognitive training program interlaced with virtual reality (VR) and mixed reality (MR) on quantitative electroencephalography (EEG) in patients with MCI. Methods: This was a 4-week, single-blind pilot study of 8 patients with MCI. The cognitive training program included art therapy, aerobic exercises, memory training, including face-name association, object recall training, and spaced retrieval; visuo-motor organization using software; similarity and ruled based categorization; and behavior modification and sequencing (e.g., making change, paying bills) using VR and MR and computerized program. Quantitative EEG was assessed on cognitive training days before treatment and after each treatment phase. Results: After one month (8 sessions) of treatment, a significant increase of delta activity over the temporal region was found compared with pretreatment values. Also, significant increases of the log EEG power were found for alpha band over the frontal and occipital regions, beta band over the frontal region and significant decreases of the log EEG power were found for theta band over the right temporal regions, and delta band over the frontal region. Significant decreases were found for Theta/beta ratio over the temporal region, for Delta/alpha ratio over the frontal region, and increase was found for Alpha/theta ratio over the occipital region. A positive correlation was found between log alpha power over the frontal and temporal regions at 4 weeks and Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) scores at 4 weeks and one month, and between log alpha power over the parieto-occipital regions and MMSE scores at 4 weeks. A negative correlation was found between log alpha power over the frontal and temporal regions at 4 weeks and baseline Alzheimer's Disease Assessment Scale-cognitive subscale scores. Conclusions: Cognitive training program interlaced with VR and MR has long-term effects on quantitative EEG in patients with MCI.
