N2  - Background: Care partners (CP) and persons with dementia (PWD) are at an increased risk of mental health issues based on grief, guilt and overall emotional burden of care. They also may have pre-existing mental health issues such as depression and anxiety, along with a lifetime history of stressors and/or trauma, of which are no less important post-dementia diagnosis. Methods: The Memory Counseling Program offers specialized supportive services including resource referrals and case collaboration with other providers. It meets the mental health needs of care partners and PWD through individual counseling, support groups, and psychoeducation. Individual counseling offers multiple modalities including SFT (solution-focused therapy), cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT), mindfulness, ACT (acceptance and commitment therapy), EMDR (eye movement desensitization and reprocessing), and counseling for couples and families, including use of the 5 Love Languages. Support groups are offered for CP of those with Alzheimer’s Disease and Frontotemporal Dementia, individuals with early to moderate stage dementia, and for couples in which one person has Mild Cognitive Impairment or early stage dementia. Education is provided through the Dementia Education Coordinator (DEC). The DEC provides individualized education sessions in the hospital setting as well as through community events such as Dementia University and Memory Cafes. Additionally, the MCP provides a dementia resource/education library with books, pamphlets, and audiovisual resources (Virtual Reality and web-based material). The MCP is a fee-for-service program, subsidized by philanthropic giving and grant-funding. Individual counseling is billed according to each client’s medical insurance and mental health benefits. Results: The MCP provides education regarding how different types of dementia affect a person, and support for the stress and burden of being a care partner, including self-care and wellness, management of behavioral disturbances and other common challenges encountered with dementia, and family meetings. Conclusions: Past history of trauma and lifetime events and an accumulation of complicated family dynamics over the years often present at this “unpacking” phase of life. Counselors provide CP and family members an opportunity to address grief, provide validation for the difficult emotions involved in being a caregiver, and provide overall support through a difficult time.
