N2  - Background: We previously found that Jak/Bondi actuarial neuropsychological criteria for mild cognitive impairment (MCI) diagnosis are adept at identifying amnestic MCI and predicting progression to Alzheimer's disease (AD). These criteria require at least two impaired test scores within a cognitive domain. The effectiveness of verbal versus visual episodic memory tests as applied to Jak/Bondi diagnostic criteria has not yet been examined. We therefore applied different combinations of memory scores to Jak/Bondi criteria to predict progression to AD in two Framingham Heart Study cohorts. Methods: 264 Original (mean age=84.6, SD=3.8) and 1175 Offspring participants (mean age=68.1, SD=5.9) were classified as MCI or cognitively normal based on Jak/Bondi criteria that applied five different combinations of verbal/visual memory tests. For each version, executive function/speed (Trail Making Test A and B) and language (Boston Naming Test and Similarities) domains were held constant. The five versions varied with respect to memory scores that were used: 1. Logical Memory (LM) Delayed Recall (DR) and LM Recognition, 2. LM DR and Visual Reproduction (VR) DR, 3. LM DR and Verbal Paired Associates (VPA) DR, 4. LM DR, VR DR, and VPA DR, 5. LM DR, LM Recognition, VR DR, and VR Recognition. Cox regressions adjusting for age, education, sex, and apolipoprotein e4 genotype examined the relative predictive utility of progression to AD. Results: MCI diagnosed by all versions of the Jak/Bondi criteria significantly predicted progression to AD. For both cohorts, individuals with amnestic MCI diagnosed by the LM DR and VR DR version had about 2-3 fold higher risk for progressing to AD (Original HR=5.977; Offspring HR=5.853) than other test combinations (HRs=1.787–4.066). As predicted, the non-amnestic MCI group HRs did not appreciably differ across versions (HRs=2.458–3.248). Inclusion of recognition memory scores did not improve the criteria's ability to predict progression to AD. Conclusions: We demonstrated that including both verbal and visual delayed recall memory scores when using Jak/Bondi criteria best predicted which individuals progressed to AD. Future studies combining both verbal and visual episodic and working memory tests may improve the predictive utility of Jak/Bondi criteria to identify individuals at risk for incident AD. [Figure presented]
