N2  - Background: Research has provided substantial evidence that care partners (CP) experience physical and emotional health declines as a direct result of their caregiving responsibilities. Educational interventions directed to CP not only reduce burden/distress, but also directly benefit persons with dementia (PWD) by increasing CP competency and self-efficacy. Yet, those that are in the most need are least serviced, such as cultural minorities the disenfranchised. Methods: The Memory Counseling Program (MCP) is an outpatient mental health clinic through Wake Forest Baptist Medical Center which provides supportive care through individual psychotherapy, group counseling, and psychoeducation. Recently, a dementia educator coordinator (DEC) position was jointly funded through a grant and philanthropic support to offer dementia-specific educational courses to reach a more diverse population of PWD and CP. The DEC is a licensed counselor and an ordained minister providing a multidisciplinary approach to education. The DEC facilitates Memory Cafés, Dementia University lectures, lunch-and-learns, and individual personalized education sessions with the use of Virtual Reality. The majority of events are held in strategic community sites to increase accessibility to those in the most need. Results: From a six month review, events led by the DEC have increased CP knowledge, increased CP coping responses, decreased CP burden, and increased CP empathy. Additionally, more diverse clients have been served through this approach than previous programming. Conclusions: The DEC provides a direct CP intervention in strategic community settings to provide additional support and empowerment to those in the most need.
