N2  - Deficits in spatial learning are early signs of Alzheimer's disease (AD). Hippocampal theta (4-12 Hz) and gamma (30-100 Hz) oscillations, as well as theta-gamma coupling, are prominent features during active spatial exploration and known to be important in spatial information processing. While a few studies have examined changes in place cell firing in mouse models of AD, many questions remain about how neural activity of different cell types in the hippocampus is altered during theta oscillations in mouse models of AD. Previously, we demonstrated gamma deficits in the 5XFAD mouse model of AD: specifically, 5XFAD mice had reduced gamma power during quiescence, when high-frequency sharp-wave ripples critical for hippocampal learning are detected. To investigate potential contributions of different cell types to hippocampal oscillatory deficits during active spatial navigation in this mouse model, we recorded local field potentials and single-unit activity with 32-channel silicon probes in the hippocampal pyramidal layer of 5XFAD mice navigating in a virtual environment. Specifically, we assessed changes in spiking activity of putative CA1 interneu-rons and pyramidal cells during theta oscillations by classifying cell types based on spike waveform properties. Our data indicate significant differences in interneuron activity between 5XFAD mice and wild-type littermates during both theta and non-theta periods. This study investigating the role of different CA1 cell types in gamma deficits may elucidate novel changes in neural activity and deficits in spatial navigation in AD.
