N2  - Background: The surgical field from the caudal side is specific to laparoscopic hepatectomy and is useful to approach to the hepatic vein during liver transection. Simulation of the surgical anatomy is important because we are not familiar with the caudal view. However, ordinary 3D analysis is observed on a two-dimensional monitor, so there is a disadvantage of lack of reality. We present laparoscopic hepatectomy simulation using VR technology which attracts attention in medical treatment in recent years. Method: A case was 77-year-old female with three metastasis from rectal cancer, and resection of segment 7 was conducted. A 3-dementional virtual hepatectomy simulation with real-time deformation (Liversim) and VR observation by a head mount display was clinically applied especially to get images of hilar portal systems and hepatic vein anatomy. Results: Preoperative 3D virtual hepatectomy was performed repeatedly on a personal computer, and we could get images of the branch of P6 and P7 in hilum and the right hepatic vein on the raw surface with deformation of the liver parenchyma. Head mounted display provides us with immersive 3D simulation. During the operation, we identified and taped the root of P7 Glisson capsule. The right hepatic vein was also identified on the raw surface from the caudal view. Laparoscopic anatomical resection of segment 7 was successfully performed. Conclusion: Simulation using VR technology is useful for laparoscopic hepatectomy from the caudal view.
