N2  - Objectives: The British Columbia (CANADA) Office of the Seniors Advocate funded a province-wide survey using a volunteer management model to conduct structured interviews with over 22K residents living in 296 care homes in the province. Learn how we: - Collected quantitative & qualitative feedback to understand quality of care from the perspective of residents in all publicly funded seniors care homes in BC - Adapted measurement methods to accommodate residents with cognitive impairment - Engaged community & clinical stakeholders to ensure project success - Communicated results to promote local and system level improvement Methods: Over 850 volunteers were screened, recruited, trained and deployed to sit with residents, often for over an hour, to engage with them, learn their life story and give them the opportunity to provide feedback about life in residential care. Volunteers from across BC participated in 128 in person and online training sessions on how to conduct structured interviews to ensure interrater reliability in administering the interRAI Self-Reported Resident Quality of Life Survey and the VR-12. In addition training included topics such as, infection prevention, privacy, communicating with people with dementia, how to use the visual analogue boards developed for the project, the survey website to register for shifts, and how to record evaluative and narrative responses. Results: Over 22 K residents in 296 facilities with publicly funded beds were approached and 9605 residents (43%) completed survey interviews with volunteers who spent over 25 K hours conducting interviews. Of the 20,354 family members who were mailed a survey 9885 responded (48%). The keys to success were the principle of inclusion with no prescreening of frail seniors for cognitive impairment, the standardized process of managing volunteers across the province at each phase of on-boarding plus common provincial branding and communications, and the investment in an online database. This resulted in a successful community engagement strategy for recruitment and deployment of volunteers and yielded high quality quantitative and qualitative data. Volunteers were asked to contribute a minimum of 30 hours; more than half exceeded this. Monthly volunteer newsletters and the survey website provided a vehicle for consistent communications and volunteer recognition. Using a volunteer workforce saved over half a million dollars versus use of paid staff. All training materials are freely available on our website for any care home wishing to repeat the survey for ongoing QI. Image: Conclusion: BC spends over $2B annually funding care homes for seniors who spend on average the last 3 years of their lives in these settings. This survey measured the quality of care from the perspective of the people who live there, and successfully engaged volunteers to engage with seniors to conduct the resident surveys. The guiding principle for the survey was “every voice counts”; every resident was invited to provide feedback to improve the quality of residential care in BC. The volunteer management model used to conduct the survey yielded not only quantitative data and qualitative “stories” from residents that will be used to inform improvements, it was a successful community engagement strategy and a cost effective approach.
