N2  - Background: Dementia Australia (DA), as the peak body representing those living with dementia, seeks to promote the use of non-pharmacological interventions to improve the quality of life and care for people living with dementia. We deliver Dementia Education with and without Virtual Reality (VR) experiences to increase knowledge, change attitude and improve care practice of carers and health professionals while also considering the care environment and the impact this has on people living with dementia. Previous research through collaboration with Swinburne University and Deakin University has shown the use of VR results in a threefold increase in building empathy and now we are conducting further comparative research. Methods: The study assessed the extent to which participants trained with VR developed increased empathy for the difficulties experienced by people diagnosed with dementia. The outcomes of VR were compared to those associated with other training methods involving VR and no virtual reality (standard training) offered by DA. • DA Training workshops compared in this study: • EDIE™ – Educational Dementia Immersive Experience – using a mobile VR headset a scenario unfolds which immerses the user in the VR setting of a person with dementia • VDE™ – Virtual Dementia Experience - purpose built room based VR which aims to emulate the sensory experience of dementia • Standard training — classroom based education • Procedure • Pre-test, post-test, follow up design. • Short structured interviews at post-test and follow up • Self-report questionnaires • Measures • Knowledge of dementia, attitudes towards dementia, empathy toward persons with dementia, and understanding of dementia care environments; Reflections on practice and the training, change of care practice, level of immersion in VR, and demographic information. • Participants o 180 professional carers Results: Analysis is still continuing, preliminary data has shown that overall, on examination of the means compared with pre-test, the post-test scores were significantly improved in all areas. The three workshop groups don't differ significantly in the improvement of their average score. Conclusions: Preliminary analysis indicates that Dementia Care Education workshops improves all measures. The full report will be available in June 2018.
