N2  - Background: Spatial orientation is a cognitive domain frequently impaired in Mild Cognitive Impairment patients and it has been associated with Alzheimer's Disease pathology. However, traditional paper-and-pencil cognitive assessments lack ecological validity to assess real-world orientation difficulties. Immersive virtual reality environments have provided new possibilities for the ecological assessment of cognitive processes, as they can reproduce sensory perceptions closer to reality. This study aims to compare the ecological validity of the original Money Road-Map Test of Direction Sense (MRMT), which provides a survey perspective of a road-map, and its immersive virtual reality maze transposition in the first-person perspective. Methods: 30 community-based participants (age 18-59, 54.8% women) were assessed using the Santa Barbara Sense of Direction Scale (SBSOD) self-report, the original paper-and-pencil MRMT and its representation using a virtual reality headset. Participants performance on both the paper and the virtual versions of the MRMT were used for correlation with SBSOD individual items and total scoring. Results: Overall, participants performed better at the paper version of the MRMT compared to the virtual task (average of correct responses (M=29.57, SD=3.036 and M=12.71, SD=11.79, for paper and virtual tasks respectively). Performance on the virtual task correlated significantly with several items of SBSOD, as well as total score (r=,630 n=29, p=,000, Spearman). Performance on the paper version, on the other hand, did not correlate with any of the SBSOD individual items, nor total score (r =-,296 n=29, p= 0,119, Spearman). Performance on both tasks correlated significantly with each other (r =,537 n=29, p= 0,002, Spearman). Conclusions: The immersive virtual reality task, in the first-person perspective, appears to assess spatial abilities closer to real-life, unlike the traditional paper-and-pencil survey evaluation. This finding supports the value of immersive virtual environments for the ecological assessments of spatial orientation and the possible advantages of its use in cognitive decline evaluations. [Figure presented]
