N2  - Background: Problems in wayfinding and spatial orientation are early symptoms of MCI and dementia. These deficits decrease mobility which again leads to further cognitive decline. In a field study (SiNDeM) we developed a pattern recognition model of disorientated behavior based on accelerometric data. There is growing evidence that impaired cognitive functioning is associated with changes in gait performance, e.g. gait variability, measured in dual-task walking conditions. Gait performance during these conditions is also a predictor of falls in old adults. But it is questionable if phases of disorientation also affect gait parameters. We aim to assess gait parameters in addition to accelerometric data during disorientation by using the GRAIL (Gait Real-Time Analysis Interactive Lab). Methods: We implemented a 3D environment of a familiar city center into the GRAIL which combines a fully instrumented treadmill with a synchronized VR environment. It allows us to analyze wayfinding behavior with simultaneous motion capture to measure gait parameters. In addition, we record accelerometric data for comparability with our field study. In the first phase of the study young and old healthy adults will participate. Phases of disorientation will be induced by changing the virtual environment. Results: First, usability and acceptance will be tested since the GRAIL was originally not constructed to measure wayfinding behavior. We pursue a participatory approach to improve VR navigation control. Results will also show if the induction of disorientation in healthy participants was successful and if this will be reflected in gait parameters. We will compare the patterns in accelerometric data with the results gained in SiNDeM. Conclusions: The GRAIL allows us to measure wayfinding behavior in a controlled and save environment, so that the paradigm is also suitable for patients. In the future it is planned to include persons with MCI and early dementia. The results will further enable the automatic detection of disorientation based on gait parameters and accelerometric data. This is necessary for the development of a situation-aware assistive system which supports persons with dementia in autonomous outdoor mobility and activities of daily living.
