N2  - Background: Falls are very common in seniors (65+). Cognitive impairment increases risk of falls. Vestibular impairment also increases with age but isn't currently part of fall-risk screens. Therefore, we tested the feasibility of a vestibular rehabilitation (VR) program in patients with a fall, cognitive impairment and detected vestibular impairment. Methods: Patients diagnosed with mild cognitive impairment or mild dementia (Alzheimer's, Vascular or mixed dementia) and a previous fall within 1 year were assessed for vestibular impairment using the Dix-Hallpike test, video Head Impulse test, Head Shake Test, the dynamic visual acuity test, and the modified clinical test of sensory interaction on balance. Those with diagnosed vestibular impairment went on to complete a baseline-assessment that included the dynamic gait index, mood, and quality of life measures. Patients included in the study were randomized to a VR or control group for 12 weeks and followed up bi-weekly by phone. Patients then returned for follow-up assessment. Feasibility parameters included recruitment rate, compliance to exercises, attrition rate, test completeness and prevalence of vestibular impairment. Results: Memory Clinic chart review revealed 424 patients that met age and mild dementia criteria. 209/422 (49.3%) had had a previous fall, 26.9% had no falls and in 23.8% no information on falls were available. Of patients with a previous fall, 48/209 (23.0%) fell in the past year, 34/48 were contacted by phone, 14/34 (41.2%) of patients contacted agreed to be screened and 12/14 attended the screen. 8/12 (75% prevalence) of screened patients had a vestibular impairment. All patients could complete the screening tests/questionnaires in their entirety. 7/8 were recruited (87.5% recruitment) and 5 patients were randomized to VR and 2 to a control group. Only 1/7 (85.7% attrition) patient attended follow-up at the 3-month mark (control arm, therefore compliance unknown). Conclusion: Vestibular impairment is prevalent in patients with cognitive impairment experiencing falls. Vestibular rehabilitation may reduce falls but is difficult to implement in patients with cognitive impairment. With the increasing prevalence of cognitive impairment, falls and vestibular impairment with age, further research is warranted to design vestibular rehabilitation for those with cognitive impairment. [Figure presented] [Figure presented] [Figure presented] [Figure presented]
