N2  - Background: Clinical trials for prevention and early intervention require measures with increased sensitivity to subtle deficits in instrumental activities of daily living (iADLs) that comprise the first functional declines in prodromal disease. The Virtual Reality Functional Capacity Assessment Tool (VRFCAT) is a performance-based assessment of iADL functioning that has demonstrated sensitivity to functional declines in individuals with subjective and objective cognitive decline. The current pilot study evaluated use of the VRFCAT in formally diagnosed patients with MCI or Mild AD. Methods: Participants included 11 individuals with MCI and 9 individuals with Mild AD. Participants completed the VRFCAT in conjunction with additional assessments of cognition and functioning including the MMSE, Clinical Dementia Rating (CDR), and partner-reported activities of daily living (ADCS-ADL_MCI). In order to assess feasibility, VRFCAT completion rates were assessed within each group. In order to assess sensitivity, VRFCAT performance for MCI and Mild AD participants was compared to normative data collected in 240 healthy individuals age 55 and older. Results: All but two participants were able to complete the VRFCAT. Based on CDR staging, these two subjects had progressed beyond mild AD at the time of the study visit (both had global CDR=2, corresponding to moderate AD). Findings demonstrated strong correlations between the VRFCAT and the MMSE (r=.8, p<.001), CDR-SB (r=.53, p<.05) and partner reported ADL functioning (r=.64, p<.01). Participants with MCI performed significantly worse than normative controls on all VRFCAT endpoints including total adjusted time, errors and forced progressions (p<.001 for all). The 7 participants with Mild AD performed significantly worse than those with MCI on all measures, performing at floor on several VRFCA tasks. Conclusions: The VRFCAT provides a sensitive tool for evaluation of iADL functioning in individuals with cognitive decline. In its current form, the VRFCAT is best suited for use in early disease, prior to the onset of frank dementia. Strong correlations with cognition and partner reported function suggest the VRFCAT may be uniquely suited for performance-based assessment of function in clinical trials in preclinical AD an MCI, as well as longitudinal investigations regarding the relationship between cognition and function.
