N2  - Objective: To compare the clinical utility of standardized memory measures with and without executive components in distinguishing cognitive complaints associated with major depressive disorder (MDD) from mild cognitive impairment (MCI). Background: MDD can produce cognitive dysfunction that mimics that of a dementia process. Despite deficits in short-term memory, patients with MDD typically show improvements in long-term recall. However, given that MDD is associated with hypoactivation in prefrontal cortical regions, we hypothesized that MDD patients would fail to exhibit improvement in delayed recall on measures with a strong executive component. Design/Methods: Twenty-two MDD and 24 MCI patients were administered measures assessing story memory (WMS-LM), simple visual memory (WMS-VR), list learning (RAVLT), and complex visual memory (ROCFT) as part of their routine clinical care. There were no significant between-group differences observed in age or education. Results: MDD patients obtained significantly higher scores than MCI patients on all memory measures, including those with [RAVLT & ROCFT: Immediate recall (p < 0.07); Delayed recall (p < 0.001] and without [WMS LM: Immediate and delayed recall (p < 0.01)] a strong executive component. MDD patients generally demonstrated improvement from immediate to delayed recall on both visual and verbal measures, with the exception of the WMS VR subtest in which a decline in performance was noted (p = 0.07). MCI patients declined from immediate to delayed recall on all memory measures, with the largest effect size observed on WMS Visual Reproduction (Cohen's d = 1.05). Conclusions: Various limitations imposed by third-party payers as well as patient factors (e.g., fatigue) preclude the use of extensive neurocognitive evaluations. The present findings suggest that under such limitations, use of the WMS-VR subtest may provide the most sensitive discrimination between patients MCI and MDD.
