N2  - Background: Engaging with art provides opportunities for persons with dementia (PWD) to express themselves creatively, improve wellbeing, and build relationships. PWD, as well as formal and informal caregivers, are more often women. In coming years, many young adults will be involved with caregiving for someone older or with dementia, yet it is unclear how much they understand the disease, and how nontraditional treatments may help. Objective(s): Responding to these unmet needs, Bringing Art to Life (BATL) partnered with Chicagoland Methodist Senior Services (CMSS) to develop BATL Chicago. BATL Chicago is a multidimensional program educating young adults about aging and dementia through didactic lecture; interviewing; immersive virtual reality (VR); and partnering them with a PWD during art therapy. Material/Methods: From September 2016 - February 2017, BATL Chicago held fourteen 195-minute colloquium sessions with twenty students (19 F, 1 M; ages 15-18) at Northside College Preparatory High School (NCP). Divided into two groups, students spent half the session in a lesson and the other half in art therapy, facilitated by an expressive art therapist, with their adult partner. Seven PWD (5 F, 2M; ages 60-92) participated. Subjects were recruited from CMSS; staff reviewed their case files to assess basic demographic and behavior characteristics. Pretests were and post-tests will be distributed to students to assess changes in empathy, attitudes toward aging, and understanding of dementia. Results: The program is ongoing. Final student pretest/posttest analysis will occur February 2017. Qualitative analysis of weekly student journal entries reveals intra-individual improvement from baseline measures of empathy and understanding. Conclusions: The BATL program is an innovative and adaptable expressive arts program partnering young adults and PWD. Preliminary findings suggest multidimensional programming including VR may be effective in increasing compassion, empathy, and understanding of aging and dementia.
