N2  - Introduction: Episodic memory is a cognitive function, uniquely different from other memory systems because of the ability to recall unique past events. During the process of recollection of the concrete event from past we need to realize at least 3 basic conditions: what, where and when happened (Allen and Fortin, 2013). This conditions form the base of the simplified model called episodic-like memory (ELM), tested also in animals. In our study, we applied this ELM model to humans using virtual reality task. Methods: The ELM task consisted of 5 trials with increasing difficulty (3, 5, 7, 9 or 11 objects). In total 70 healthy human volunteers were tested in the virtual ELM task, asked to remember temporal sequence and spatial positions of several objects. We compared two virtual environments: A) Office (n= 40) a large unstructured open space with two floors; B) House (n= 30) a smaller well-structured space divided into 7 rooms. We calculated the error rates both in the temporal order and spatial positions of individual objects. In addition, we aimed to compare the effect of the applied environment on tested performance. Results: Our results show that number of both type of errors increased with increasing task difficulty in both environments. Volunteers showed increased ability to remember sequences and positions of objects in the structured House environment. In addition, while the structured environment showed lower number of position than order errors, the open space did not demonstrate such difference. Discussion: Preliminary results show that the structured virtual environment is more suitable for recollection of past experiences. Debriefing of the participants helped us to identify variable strategies used by the participants to solve the task, e.g. story creating, repeating the order or position of objects during their collection. The novel virtual ELM task is crucial for future clinical studies due to its ecological validity and dependence on integrity of hippocampal structure and its connectivity that could be affected in disorders like Alzheimer dementia or Schizophrenia.
