N2  - Background: Virtual reality (VR), a simulated computer-generated environment, is increasingly being considered in terms of its application to the assessment of dementia. Complementary evidence suggests that a person's physical and verbal reactions within social VR scenarios can be considered a relative proxy of social cognition in the real world. We report on the development of VR social scenarios designed to elicit evidence of impairment in different components of social cognition in individuals with behavioural variant frontotemporal dementia (bvFTD) and a typical Alzheimer's disease (tAD) control group relative to healthy participants. Methods: Data from 10 individuals with bvFTD, 10 with tAD and 20 healthy volunteers will be reported. Participants are immersed in the 5-minute virtual scene of a cafe bistro. The scenarios are manipulated in terms of the visual and social distinctiveness of the customers (conventional/ unconventional characters), valence (positive/negative) and the extent to which the participant is privy to social exchanges within the scene (e.g. direct, where characters interact within the scene, vs. indirect, where the anchor character receives valenced information over the telephone). The virtual scenario also includes a 'slapstick' style accident involving the anchor character. Observational measures include verbal and physical reactions, location and proximity data, direction of gaze and pre-/post-immersion structured interviews. VR data will be compared with scores on pen-and-paper neuropsychological tests of social cognition and carer reports of behaviour. Results: Comparisons between the bvFTD, tAD and control groups in their verbal, physical and oculomotor reactions to the virtual scenarios will be reported. Early data indicate marked differences between bvFTD patients and controls not only in responses to primary characters and social scenarios but also in exploratory behaviour and responses to background characters and elements of the scenario. Conclusions: We will discuss the utility of VR scenarios for eliciting evidence of social cognitive deficits in individuals with bvFTD and other dementia syndromes. Future work will evaluate the sensitivity and specificity of VR social cognition tasks relative to traditional clinical report and paper-and-pencil neuropsychological measures in detecting social cognition changes in individuals at risk of familial FTD before the onset of frank behaviour change.
