N2  - Objectives: Research by the Dutch Alzheimer Association showed that 82% of the informal carers of people with dementia are severely burdened or at risk of overburdening. Into D'mentia (ID) was developed to promote the understanding and awareness of the experience of people with dementia, aiming to reduce carer burden and promoting more empathetic care. The ID intervention consists of a simulator in a mobile container using virtual reality and serious gaming techniques, by means of which participants experience the impact of dementia in daily life situations. In addition to the simulator, one week later participants are offered a short group training (half a day). This method makes for a truly personal experience and understanding of dementia possible, since every carer invited into ID will cope with the disabilities they are confronted with differently, and will feel touched or stressed by different events in the simulator. ID accurately and recognizably portrays a day in the life of someone with dementia. The development of ID was based on real experiences of people with dementia as described in the literature and explored in field work. In our presentation the ID will be presented and its development will be explained and discussed. Methods: Comprehensive research was done, first mapping experiences of people with dementia from literature, and then discussing these with people with dementia, carers, and professionals in the Netherlands to develop a fully recognizable scenario for this experience. For mapping experiences, the literature was reviewed for citations of people with dementia on how they experience their dementia. Citations were categorized in themes (adaptation aspects, problem areas, quality of life domains), which were then discussed in separate focus groups with Dutch persons with dementia and informal carers, living in different regions of the country, to determine if they recognised these experiences and how it influenced their lives. Additionally, dementia experts were asked in a survey to rate the frequency of the occurrence of the mentioned experiences as well as to appraise the impact of the inventoried problems on the daily lives of people with dementia. Results: Ten articles were found, which stated 257 different quotes from people with dementia, from interviews, questionnaires or writing. These quotes were categorized into either an adaptive task area of the adaption-coping model; a domain of Quality of Life (QOL) or one of the problem areas mentioned by carers in the Dutch National Dementia Programme. After coding and categorizing, all relevant domains and quotes were discussed in two focus groups with people with dementia (n = 19) and two with informal carers (n = 15). The domains that were recognized most by participants in the focus groups were those in the domain of adaptive tasks, most specifically the theme 'dealing with own disability' and 'maintaining a positive self-image': many participants indicated that they recognized having to deal with disabilities in their memory, and found ways to make up for that. They also described how important it was to them to make themselves useful to others, which was the most recognized item of the Quality of Life domains. Informal carers confirmed the domains that were mentioned by people with dementia, thus emphasizing the importance of coping with disabilities as the most important area. Based on this literature survey and field work, a realistic scenario was written for ID. Conclusion: The Into D'mentia simulator was evaluated on user friendliness and usefulness among users and rated as a highly useful and very user friendly innovation (see Hattink et al., 2015). The results of this evaluation were used to fine tune the further development of the simulator.
