N2  - Background: Memory training can be a powerful approach to improve memory and promote compensatory brain plasticity in the prodromal phase of Alzheimer's disease. Methods: Twenty older adults with subjective cognitive decline (SCD) received three onehour sessions of group memory training where they learned to use the Method of loci and apply it to a virtual reality environment. Behavioral efficacy was measured using cognitive tasks and performance in a virtual shopping task to measure transfer. Trainingrelated brain changes were measured with structural and functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI). Results: Results indicate improved word list learning following training. fMRI of encoding- related activation indicated post-training increase in the right fusiform and in the left superior and middle frontal gyri. Thus, these regions, which were activated prior to training, showed an increase in their activation following training. There were also new activations in the right hippocampus and left superior parietal lobule, i.e., activations that were absent prior to training but present following training. Conclusions: These results are interpreted within the Interactive model for training-induced brain plasticity. Following strategy-based memory training, persons with SCD showed increased reliance on brain regions specialized in memory encoding, but also appeared to recruit new alternative regions they had not recruited prior to training. The training may also have a restorative impact. For instance, participants showed no hippocampal activation in this memory task before training but showed activation in the right hippocampus following training.
