N2  - Background: The increasing number of people living with post-stroke sequels has stimulated the search for novel tools to improve the efficacy of the post stroke rehabilitation. Common upper extremity impairments after stroke include paresis, loss of fractionated movement, abnormal muscle tone and/or changes in sensitivity. Virtual reality has been proposed as a novel therapeutic tool for motor rehabilitation. Among the virtual reality devices with potential for use in hemiparetic upper limb rehabilitation, the Nintendo Wii TM (NW) stands out as a portable, easy-to-use, low-cost alternative that offers good reliability. However, the transferring of the motor gains obtained by game training to other activities needs be more investigated because this is an essential requirement for using in rehabilitation. Purpose: To verify the transferring of the gains achieved by training with NW games performed with upper extremity to joint range of motion, movement velocity and coordination of the paretic upper limb in patients with chronic sequels of stroke (CSS). Methods: The experimental group comprised 20 individuals with CSS with a mean age of 66.4 years (S.D. = 7.14), mean time of Stroke years (S.D. = 7.14), asymptomatic for depression and dementia. A total of 4 games were selected on the NW that elicited large, fast and symmetric upper limb movement. Games were performed sitting on the NWF platform, using the Wii Remote and Nunchuk in the paretic upper limb and guided by visual stimuli projected into a screen placed from the patient. The training was split into on session per week where each game was repeated for 5 time. Additionally, there was one more session to test the retention, performed, 7 days after the end of training. The learning and retention were evaluated by mean scores in the games. The transferring was evaluated by computerizing evaluation system for joint range of motion, movement velocity and coordination. All measures were evaluated in three assessment time points: before training (BT); immediately (AT), 48 hours (48hAT) and 7 days after the end of training (7sAT). Results: Four RM-ANOVA, one for each game, using as factor assessment time points (BT, AT, 48hAT and 7dAT) as repeated measures, showed a significant of the assessment points for all games (p < .01, ES > 80). Tukey pos-hoc test (TT) showed a significant increase in the scores, remained at the end of the study, for all games. Three RM-ANOVA using as factor assessment time points (BT, AT, 48hAT and 7dAT) as repeated measures for joint range of motion, movement velocity and coordination showed a significant effect of the assessment points for all measures (p < .01, ES < .70). Tukey pos-hoc test (TT) f showed a significant increase in all the measures, remained at the end of the study, excluding the range of motion in the should abduction. Conclusion(s): Patients with chronic sequels of stroke showed a significant learning, retention and, most important, transferring of the gain obtained by training in NWF games. Implications: The Nintendo Wii can be considered a useful tool to improve joint range of motion, movement velocity and coordination of the paretic upper limb, even in patients with chronic stroke.
