N2  - Background: The increasing number of people living with post-stroke sequels has stimulated the search for novel tools to improve the efficacy of the post stroke rehabilitation. Stroke causes sensory-motor impairment, leading to balance problems, falls, and functional limitations, which result in the incapacity to perform different tasks of daily living. Body asymmetry and difficulty to transfer the weight to the paretic side affect the capacity to maintain postural control. Virtual reality has been proposed as a novel therapeutic tool for motor rehabilitation. Among the virtual reality devices with potential for use in balance rehabilitation, the Nintendo Wii FIT™ (NWF) stands out as a portable, easy-to-use, low-cost alternative that offers good reliability. However, the transferring of the motor gains obtained by game training to other activities needs be more investigated because this is an essential requirement for using in rehabilitation. Purpose: To verify the transferring of the gains achieved by training with NWF games to postural control in patients with chronic sequels of stroke (CSS). Methods: The experimental group comprised 14 individuals with CSS, 8 male, mean age of 51.71 years (S.D. = 10.43), mean time of Stroke 4.47 years (S.D. = 4.06), 9 left paretic side, asymptomatic for depression and dementia. A total of 5 games were selected on the NWF that elicited slow and fast movements from the center of gravity, associated or otherwise with upper limb movement, and step alternation under different conditions. Games were performed on a platform and guided by visual stimuli projected onto a screen placed from the patient. The training was split into one session per week where each game was repeated for 5 times. Additionally, there was one more session to test the retention, performed 7 days after the end of training. The leaning and retention were evaluated by mean scores in the games. The transferring was evaluated by tests to assess balance and postural control on force plate (Balance Master Neurocom™). All measures were evaluated in three assessment time points: before training (BT); immediately (AT), 48 hours (48hAT) and 7 days after the end of training. Results: Five RM-ANOVA, one for each game, using as factor assessment time points (BT, AT, 48hAT, 7dAT) as repeated measures, showed a significant of the assessment points for all games (p < .01, ES > 80). Tukey pos-hoc test (TT) showed a significant increase in the scores, remained at the end of the study, for all games. RM-ANOVA using as factor assessment time points (BT,AT, 48hAT, 7dAT) as repeated measures showed a significant effect of the assessment points for velocity, symmetry and length of step (p < .01, ES < .70); limits of stability for paretic side and rhythmic weight shift. Tukey pos-hoc test showed a significant increase in all of these measures, remained at the end of the study. Conclusion(s): Patients with chronic sequels of stroke showed a significant learning, retention and, most important, transferring of the gain obtained by training in NWF games. Implications: The Nintendo Wii can be considered a useful tool to improve balance and postural control even in patients with chronic stroke.
