N2  - Background: Physiotherapy has techniques and strategies for treatment of these conditions in order to delay disease progression, improve vestibular function and quality of life of the affected patient. The aim of this study is to explore the effects of a physiotherapy intervention aimed Vestibular Rehabilitation (VR) in elderly patients with PD. Purpose: Explore the effects of a physiotherapy intervention aimed Vestibular Rehabilitation (VR) in elderly patients with PD. Methods: This work was developed from a literature review, the 2008 to 2013 period, which includes related to Parkinson's disease research in the elderly, and treatments and physiotherapy resources used in this type of dementia. An online search was conducted in the following scientific databases: SciELO, LILACS, MEDLINE, and the Treaty of Geriatrics and Gerontology. To search for studies on the subject, were used separately in the following terms: Parkinson's disease, physical therapy, vestibular rehabilitation and elderly. Results: The age factor is not considered limiting the response to treatment in the RV, claims the study of Ricci, Aratani, owner, Macedo, and Gananc¸a Caovilla (2010), where the exercises were customized with patients, being performed at the clinic and at home daily and was found to improve the sensory conflict in elderly patients with dizziness and imbalance corporal6. According to Santos, Peracini, Franco Nogueira and Souza (2012), the improvement reported by patients regarding the use of FNP in PD showed up with respect to the basic clinical picture of patients: bradykinesia, rigidity and gait. But we note a gap in the literature involving the use of it in DP7. In the study of ZEIGELBOIM, Klagenberg, Teive, Muñoz-Bassetto and Martins (2009) in Curitiba-PR, the RV showed good results for patients with PD through the properties of the exercises, which play an important role in vestibular function, contributing to the reduction of sensory conflict in these elderly, promoting improved on balance, favoring the performance of daily living activities, and for promoting a better quality of life for the elderly with DP8. Conclusion(s): Through this study, it is found that the exercises and physiotherapy resources in the RV are of great importance and act along with drug treatment in PD. When properly oriented to physical therapy for elderly patients, treatment becomes extremely important to maintain, improve and extend the quality of life of the individual affected. Fitting the therapist to draw the best approach based on evaluation and complaints of the elderly patient, in order to support the functionality and prevent the motor disabilities. Implications: When well prepared the physical therapy protocol, the literature shows that physical therapy can promote significant improvement in vestibular function, reducing the signs and symptoms of the patient, providing a better quality of life.
