N2  - Background: The importance of early intervention in dementia cannot be over stated. Pharmacological interventions have often been the mainstay of treatment, but conscious effort should be made for non-pharmacological interventions. Technology is becoming pervasive in the everyday life of people worldwide. The comfort with which many embrace it, present an opportunity for exploring it for therapeutic intervention.In this study, the applicability of a 3D virtual environment as a possible non-pharmacological intervention in early dementia was evaluated. Methods: A novel 3D rehabilitation platform was developed to simulate daily activities. It featured 2 environments; a supermarket and a household kitchen. The participants interact with the virtual environments using 3D-glasses and a modified controller. Scenarios are presented and participants navigate through the environment with guidance from a psychologist. Participants are first introduced to the concept of 3D, trained on the equipment and acquainted with the environment. Specific tasks administered, requiring attention, reasoning and problem solving, tap on various cognitive domains including episodic memory, working memory, visuospatial orientation and psychomotor ability. The 45 minute session is punctuated intermittently to monitor participant's comfort level to 3D imagery. Feedback is collated with participants assessing 1) efficacy of the technology for the intended group, and 2) effectiveness of the session in cognitive stimulation. Results:A pilot evaluation was conducted with 8 participants; 5 with mild dementia and 3 cognitively normal; ages 58years to 82years (Mean age =73.9 years), 75% were Males. 87.5% of the participants acknowledged the realism of the 3D and 75% felt at ease in navigating the environment after the initial practice. 1 cognitively normal participant experienced discomfort which she attributed to a history of vertigo. Remaining participants (87.5%) reported the session interesting and75% would return for future session. All acknowledged the intended benefit of the 3D rehabilitation program in cognitive stimulation and would recommend it. Conclusions: The 3D rehabilitation tool as an effective therapeutic intervention in early dementia has potential. The experiential learning on the program simulates reality without the fear and embarrassment of cognitive lapses. Bridging the technology divide is the psycho-social support from the psychologist. Further evaluation of the program is currently in progress.
