N2  - Elderly individuals often report a decline in navigational abilities such as finding one’s way in a novel environment and planning routes to distant locations. These problems can have a severe impact on everyday life, compromising an individual’s independence. At present, surprisingly little is known about the precise nature of such impairments because previous research in this field is relatively scarce and often remains at a descriptive level without providing a detailed understanding of the neural mechanisms that mediate age-related navigational impairments. The research program of the newly established Aging & Cognition Research Group at the German Centre for Neurodegenerative Diseases (DZNE) in Magdeburg aims to address this issue with a novel approach to cognitive ageing that is dee-ply rooted in animal neurobiology and closely informed by anatomical studies and computational modelling. Spatial navigation relies on a network of brain structures that are known to be particularly sensitive to the aging process. For example, layer II of the entorhinal cortex, where grid cells with distinct spatial correlates have been observed, appears to be particularly sensitive to the deleterious consequences of aging. Specifically, neurodegenerative changes in this region, such as the neuronal loss that occurs in the earliest stages of age-related cognitive decline and Alzheimer’s disease, may trigger downstream functional decrements in the hippocampus. The fine-grained spatial resolution (voxel size: 0.35 mm in-plane) of the 7T MRI scanner at the Leibniz Institute for Neurobiology in Magdeburg will enable our group to reliably identify the various hippocampal subfields and the deep, middle and superficial layers in the entorhinal cortex. Moreover, by combining ultra-high field brain imaging with cutting-edge virtual environment technology and innovative data analysis techniques, we will distinguish the neural representations and computational mechanisms associated with these subregions. Importantly, this research program has the potential to reveal general mechanisms of age-related changes in neural information processing that could also have implications for nonspatial functions. In this poster we present the overall aim of our newly established research group, specific proposed lines of inquiry, proposed methodologies, and a discussion of the potential impact of our results.
