N2  - The proceedings contain 165 papers. The special focus in this conference is on learning and memory with processes of language and intellectual competence. The topics include: Interactions of working and long-term memory in the medial temporal lobe; ICT and brain training affect positively on selected age related cognitive changes; neural activation changes during acquisition of a bimanual tracking task under augmented and nonaugmented feedback conditions in elderly; older adults’ social source memory is quite good, but less flexible than that of young adults; reward-based learning in healthy aging — deficits and spared aspects; benefits of exercise training intervention on attentional control and quality of life in healthy and frail older adults; a comparison of multi-domain and single-domain cognitive trainings in old age; prefrontal neural activation during dual-task walking in old-age; the effect of blood-brain barrier function on cognition; cognitive ergonomic design of project management software for aging users; a brain potential analysis of short-latency cognitive compensation in early-stage ad; depression and depressive symptoms in ageing; aging magnifies the effects of COMT gene on cognitive flexibility; aging magnifies the genetic (C957T-DRD2) impact on inhibitory control; ERP evidence for life-span differences in the use of proactive and reactive cognitive control; electrophysiological studies of cognitive functions in healthy aging and cognitive decline; the role of modality on correct recognition and misinformation in younger and older adults; underlying mechanisms of useful field of view and insight visual speed of processing training; different effects of different physical training programs on cognitive function in older men; the effect of age and of the tonality of the induced emotion on error negativity (Ne); performance monitoring in aging; structural brain imaging correlates of memory training in middle and old age; age-related impairments in learning to predict future reward; neurophysiological approaches towards an earlier diagnosis of Alzheimer’s disease; age-related changes in feedback processing; impact of cardiovascular health and depressive symptoms on cognitive ability in healthy aging; top-down control of attention and aging; training effects on P3 potential in a task-switching paradigm; the impact of financial incentives on motor learning in younger and older age; decision making and error monitoring in elderly employees; the impact of interference on working memory and long-term memory in aging; changes in functional networks in the aging brain; control of spatial attention in aging; hindsight bias and aging; neural reference-ability networks across the life span; adult age differences in the attentional control of representational distinctiveness; using distraction to facilitate remembering in older adults; on the application of explicit strategies in visuomotor adaptation; differential mechanisms for the development of prospective memory across the lifespan; modeling the effects of aging on prospective-memory monitoring; the processing capacity requirements of maintaining upright posture; subjective memory impairment at the pre-dementia stage of Alzheimer’s disease; age-related differences in filtering, attentional control, and working memory capacity; improving executive control in younger and older adults; the neural correlates of emotional arousal and valence processing in healthy ageing; development of prospective memory in old age; neural substrates of rhythmic timing in young and older professional musicians and controls; the role of psychological well-being concerning bright light effects on cognitive performance; age-related associative memory deficits for verbal and visual stimuli; functional approaches to cognitive interventions; age differences in decisions under risk; increased reactivation aversion effect in old age; the mismatch negativity as an index of normal and abnormal brain aging; prediction of cognitive decline in elders at risk for AD; cognitive outcomes of white matter hyperintensities in normal aging and late life depression; combined effects of aging and cognitive control on brain oscillations in a task-switching protocol; a natural history of elderly people in the north of England; effects of aging on planning and execution of coordinated eye and hand movements; healthy aging tips the balance from goal-directed action to S-R habit acquisition; a modeling approach to elucidate individual parameters of central and neuromuscular processes; when words are losing their meaning — aging related changes in central auditory processing; age-related changes in working memory; individual differences in training and transfer effects; ERP-correlates of context updating in younger and older adults; successful aging in the workplace; examining age-related effects in visual working memory; spectral fingerprints of large-scale neuronal interactions; age effects on the functional connectivity of the motor network during the resting state and motor control abilities; aging, directed forgetting, and working memory; rehabilitating older ears and older brains; a brain potential analysis of lifespan changes in response activation and inhibition; a meta-analysis of age-related speed differences in fifteen elementary tasks; differential effects of endurance and coordination training on cortical and subcortical brain structures; rapid mental fatigue amplifies age-related attentional deficits; effects of omega-3 supplementation on brain structure and function in healthy elderly subjects; spatial navigation — a unique window into mechanisms of cognitive ageing; synchronization of brain oscillations and aging; the effects of aging on white matter and cognitive rhythms; disrupting the ipsilateral motor cortex interferes with training of a complex motor task in older adults; cognitive and neural effects induced by an object-location memory training in old age; individual differences in forgetting in old age; the neural correlates of recollection and familiarity during aging; inhibitory control in older adults’ episodic memory; possible effects of a simulated altitude training program on cognitive performance; a study of the praxic semantic system; serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D is not associated with domain-specific cognitive performance in Dutch elderly; age diversity and group-level stereotypes as interactive predictors of conflicts; it’s better to drop that glass before you stumble; the recollective experience in web-based interaction; change in intra-individual variability in older adults; the effect of age on the mixing and switching cost asymmetry; remembering locations — localization performance in older adults; reduced auditory distraction in athletic, than non-athletic seniors; differential involvement of executive control and representations; age-related differences in performance and stimulus processing in dual task situation; a unique window to study cognitive aging; adult age differences in prospective memory; age-differences in memory-based decisions; assessment and training of cognitive functions in young and older subjects using a novel virtual reality application; benefits of healthy elderly in selective attention under consideration of perceptual load; shifts of attention near the hand; self-regulatory aptitudes of older drivers; factors associated with depression among elderly in Karachi Pakistan; the effects of brain fitness on auditory and cognitive outcomes; semantic richness influences object naming in old age; working memory related changes in brain activity and functional connectivity in the aging brain; improving working memory capacity by training attentional filtering; age differences in prospective remembering of everyday-life intensions; executive functions and falls in older adults; a comparison between office and industrial workers; working memory updating training in older adults; age-related differences in basic motor skills reflect the impact of the death of handwriting; aging and organisation in episodic memory; effects of aging on neural bases of strategy selection; cognitive terminal decline in very old age; the impact of 24 weeks protein supplementation on cognitive performance in frail elderly; feedback-related processing in healthy aging and patients with Parkinson’s disease; physical activity and attentional control in middle-aged adults and age-differences in peripheral signal-detection revealed by event-related potentials.
