N2  - Background: Human spatial navigation involves landmark recognition, egocentric route following, directional sense, cognitive mapping, path integration, and problem solving ability. Impairment of any of the mentioned abilities will result in wayfinding difficulty or even getting lost. From clinical viewpoint, spatial navigation impairment (SNI) may occur in people with Alzheimer disease (PwAD) in the very early stage or in places they know very well such as their own neighborhoods or community. More often than not, it can be one of the incipient symptoms of AD which occurs months or years before the emergence of other cognitive deficits. Aim: In this talk, the presenter will introduce several unique findings observed by our research team regarding the mechanisms of spatial navigation impairment in PwAD (mild). Method and findings: These patients, in fact, have normal abilities to produce and use personally familiar cognitive maps when assessed by virtual reality arena. They are, however, impaired in the recognition of familiar landmarks of theirs, in particular in a twilight or blurred condition. Moreover, young onset PwAD have more difficulties choosing direction in the crossroads. The under-appraisal or poor awareness of the SNI in PwAD may result in their repetitive getting lost. We have developed a questionnaire to assess everyday navigational ability (QuENA). A two and half-year follow-up study to predict incidence and recurrence of getting lost in a group of community dwelling PwAD will be reported. To improve traffic safety and quality of life of the PwAD, all health providers are welcome to apply the QuENA onto their patients. The QuENA is upon request at pair@mail.ncku.edu.tw. Conclusion: The mechanisms for SNI are complex. The impairment in early visuoperceptual processes might play a critical role and the association between landmarks and the destination may also be disrupted. Based on the brainbehavioral relationship, the role of SNI as a marker for AD is promising.
