N2  - Background: Sleep disturbances are considered one of the most relevant symptoms in the premotor phase in Parkinson's disease (PD). Aims: a. To evaluate the impact of sleep disturbances on mental status in non demented PD patients b. To test a dedicated virtual reality to detect those dysexecutive dysfunctions hidden to the neuropsychological test and correlate with polysonnographyc findings. Methods: a. We selected 30 nondemented PD patients by a dedicated neuropsychological battery. All these patients performed a polisomnographyc study, within 7 days after the neuropsychological exam. Even if neuropsychological findings exclude a cognitive impairment, most of these patients may present “under the mean” scores in isolated cognitive items. b. We used a Virtual Reality version of the Multiple Errand Test (VMET) in order to evaluate decision making ability in PD notdemented patients. The MET is an assessment of executive functions in daily life and consists to perform tasks according predefined rules (www.neurovr.org). Results: Our findings show a significant correlation between presence of an alteration of N-REM structure and lower scores of FAB and Digit span. In PD patients, abnormalities in the polysonnographyc recordings resulted correlated with a defective strategy during the execution of tasks in the virtual supermarket. Conclusions: Neurodegenerative process can involve common anatomofunctional pathways regulating both N-REM sleep structures and some executive functions. Sleep benefit deprivation may induce in the daytime altered cognitive performances, which can further progress in a dysexecutive syndrome. VMET data reported that also PD not-demented patients may show an underlying unknown cognitive dysfunction.
