N2  - Introduction: Memory for facts and events (declarative memory) is severely compromised in disorders affecting the medial temporal lobe (MTL) such as Alzheimer's disease (AD). Within the MTL, the entorhinal cortex (ERC) provides major input into the hippocampus via the perforant path and is affected early in AD. Novel therapeutic strategies could be developed if stimulation enhanced declarative memory in a reliable and specific manner. Methods: We performed a study involving seven neurosurgical patients implanted with intracranial depth electrodes to identify seizure- onset zones for potential curative resection. Patients completed spatial learning and perceptuo motor control tasks during unilateral electrical stimulation of hippocampus or ERC/perforant pathway. During the spatial learning task, patients explored virtual environments where they learned various destinations. During half of the spatial learning and control trials stimulation was given below threshold to elicit an after-discharge and be detectable by patient. Results: Unilateral stimulation of the ERC/perforant pathway produced a facilitative effect on memory expressed as significant shortening of the route required to reach the destination. Effects were substantial (mean improvement = 70%; t = -3.2807, p < 0.05; Cohen's d = 1.74) and specific to the learning task, as stimulation did not enhance performance on perceptuo-motor tasks. Stimulation effects were regionally specific; memory was not enhanced with hippocampal stimulation. ERC/perforant pathway stimulation resulted in hippocampal theta resetting, a mechanism thought to allow for optimal learning. Conclusions: Stimulation of the ERC/perforant pathway enhanced learning in neurosurgical patients. Overall, these results raise the possibility that deep brain stimulation of the ERC could be utilized to enhance memory performance in clinical conditions where memory is impaired.
