N2  - Background:Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)markers of brain aging and performance on memory and executive function tests are powerful predictors of dementia. Our aim was to test association of vascular risk factor exposure in midlife with progression of MRI-markers of brain aging and measures of cognitive decline. Methods: 1,352 non-demented Framingham Offspring Study participants were studied. Multivariable linear and logistic regressions were implemented to study the association of midlife vascular risk factor exposure with longitudinal change in white matter hyper intensity volume (WMHV), total brain volume (TBV), temporal horn volume (THV), logical memory delayed-recall (LM-d), visual reproductions delayed-recall (VR-d) and Trail-Making-Test B-A(TrB-A) performance a decade later. Results: Hypertension and systolic blood pressure in midlifewere significant predictors of WMHV progression (regression coefficient 6 standard error: 0.2360.06, p< 0.001 0.00660.002, p< 0.001) and worsening executive function (TrB-A score;-0.1760.08, p=0.012; -0.00560.002, p=0.002). Midlife diabetes and smoking were associatedwith a more rapid increase in temporal horn volume, a surrogate marker of accelerated hippocampal atrophy (0.2760.11, p=0.017; 0.1960.07, p=0.008). Midlife smoking also predicted a more marked decrease in total brain volume (-0.1560.07, p=0.025) and increased risk of extensive change in WMHV (odds ratio [OR]=1.58 [95% confidence interval [CI]:1.07-2.33], p=0.021). Obesity in midlife was associated with an increased risk of being in the top quartile of change in executive function (OR=1.39[1.02-1.88], p=0.035). Longitudinal changes in brain structure were significantly correlated with decline in memory and executive function. Conclusions: Midlife hypertension, diabetes, smoking and obesity increased the rate of progression of vascular brain injury, global and hippocampal atrophy and decline in executive function a decade later.
