N2  - Introduction: The lack of reliable standards complicates the validation of methods for analyzing amyloid brain PET scan, and investigators have relied on theoretical modeling and clinical diagnoses as surrogates for true pathology. We will report the results from the Phase III multicenter validation study validating three methods for assessment florbetapir F18 PET (18F-AV45) amyloid images: a semiquantitative visual read, a mean SUVr quantitation based on VOIs, and a novel histogram based quantitative approach by comparing amyloid burden by postmortem histopathology assays. Methods: Subjects with a life expectancy of < 6 months had a 10 minute PET scan 50 minutes after IV injection of 370MBq of florbetapir F18. Images were reconstructed by iterative technique with 4i/16s and 3 mm FWHM Gaussian filter. The images were visually assessed (VR) in a 5 point scale comparing cerebellum and cortical uptake. For the SUVr technique, PET images were spatially normalized by 12 parameter affine registration to Talairach/MNI space and counts extracted from six regional VOIs (frontal, temporal, precuneus, parietal, anterior and posterior cingulate) and SUVr calculated with reference to Cerebellum. For the histogram based analysis image data were processed automatically to generate a 128 bin count histogram. The first order derivative convolved histogram curve was searched in the intensity direction to estimate separation of high/low intensity areas and ratio calculated (CAR) to estimate specific binding. After the patient's death brain was evaluated for β-amyloid deposition using immunohistochemistry (IHC) and by neuritic plaque rating (modified CERAD). CAR score, VR score and SUVr were compared with IHC/CERAD using Spearman's (p) or Pearson's correlation (r). Results: Strong correlation was obtained between measures of PET amyloid binding and amyloid deposition at autopsy on 6 subjects (3 men; 3 women; mean age 76y; clinical diagnosis: 1 MCI, 4 AD, 1 PDD, mean interval from imaging to autopsy 43 days), (VR and IHC (p=0.88), VR and CERAD (p =0.92); SUVr and IHC (r=0.89), SUVr and CERAD (p=0.93); CAR and IHC (r=0.97), CAR and CERAD (p =0.64)). Full results for the Phase III trial on the 35 subjects will be presented. Conclusions: The results confirm that florbetapir F18 PET imaging provides relevant in vivo estimate of brain amyloid pathology irrespectively of the choice of image assessment method. The novel histogram based method is particularly interesting because it is quantitative and does not require fitting images to a template or kinetic data acquisition, input functions or drawing/applying regions of interest.
