N2  - Introduction: Teaching medical trainees to perform home safety assessment helps students recognize unsafe conditions in the home for patients with dementia and falls; however, conducting this training in patient homes is resource intensive. Virtual worlds offer inexpensive and safe 3D environments for learners to train in identifying safety hazards. Our aim was to evaluate the feasibility and acceptability of 3D Interactive Home Simulation and to correlate performance with spatial ability, self-efficacy and presence (sense of being in the virtual environment). Methods: Using a 3D virtual home, we conducted a pilot correlation study to evaluate usability and utility. An expert panel of home care physicians generated an inventory of potential safety hazards and created a 60-item inventory checklist (Cronbach's alpha of 0.86). These hazards were replicated in a virtual house in the computerbased 3D virtual world Second Life (Linden Labs). Investigators instructed thirty medical trainees to identify as many hazards as possible using a think-aloud protocol. After the intervention participants completed a usability and cognitive load survey followed by mental rotation test and presence questionnaire. Results: The mean age of participants was 29 yrs+/-3. The subjects mean score + SD on performance was 66%+15. Confidence in identifying home safety hazards (utility) improved significantly from a pretest median of 4+1 to a posttest of 6+1 on a 7 point Likert scale (p< 0.05). Performance was highly correlated to visual spatial ability r =0.7 p< 0.05. Presence also correlated with performance r(n=30) =0.41 p< 0.05, while cognitive load did not. Conclusion: Virtual worlds offer a cost-effective useful and usable alternative to a real world home safety evaluation by medical trainees. Educators need to consider visual spatial ability when implementing virtual environments.
