N2  - Background: Even if Parkinson's disease (PD) is thought to be primarly a movement disorder due to a dysfunction of the nigrostriatal dopaminergic system, in the last years, many studies, supported by clinical evidences, have revealed an alteration of executive functions. This includes decrements in planning, concept formation, working and visual memory, attention deficits, difficulty in dual and sequencing tasks. Virtual Reality (VR) immersion works as an experimental model where “real” motor symptoms are eliminated or attenuated, and thus focusing on “pure” cognitive performances. Aim: Scope of our study was to evaluate planning, memory, attention, dual-sequencing tasks abilities in PD non-demented patients. Methods: We studied 10 non-demented patients with PD and 10 controls by a virtual reality (VR) version of the Multiple Errand Test (VMET), which is an assessment of executive functions in daily life. It is performed in a shopping mall-like setting where there are items to be bought and information to be obtained. Thus, in a virtual supermarket, subjects were requested to select and buy various products presented on shelves with the aid of a joy pad. Results: The execution time was higher in controls than patients; while concerning errors in executing the task, the mean rank for patients was significantly higher than for control subjects. Conclusions: Impulsive deficit control may involve planning and decision making in PD, inducing to short time and full of errors strategies. These data suggest the idea that in PD behavioural disturbances can progressively induce to cognitive dysfunctions.
