N2  - Rehabilitation of cognitive functions after cortical lesions represents a great challenge.Perception, attention,memory, language andother cognitive functions consist of distributed interactive and overlapping networks. Multisensory influences are integral to primary as well as higher order cortical operations. The healthy human brain has a large capacity for automatic simultaneous processing and integration of sensory information. Cortical lesions interrupt the horizontal networks that combine different regions and the capacity for automatic processing of incoming stimuli is reduced. Problems in perception, attention andmemory are frequent in addition to specific deficits in language and in spatial awareness (neglect). There is substantial evidence for a close coupling between motor activity and speech. Listening to speech specifically modulates tongue muscles, and language perception activates the hand motor cortex. Audiovisual speech perception activates a network of brain regions that include cortical motor areas involved in planning and executing speech production. The intensity in the training is a key factor in aphasia and environmental encouragement is necessary for transforming the effects obtained in specific language training into useful verbal communication. Virtual reality and transcranial magnetic stimulation has been shown to have some effect on neglect, andTMS togetherwith sometraining programsmight also have an effect on other problems. There is a great need for more research in this field.
