N2  - Objective: The purpose of this study was to examine the impact of cardiac disease on the cognitive profile of patients with Alzheimer's disease (AD). Method: Subjects were 44 patients with AD, 46 patients with AD + cardiac disease (ADC), and 35 patients with vascular dementia (VaD). Average age and education of the overall sample was 80.03 (7.60) and 12.50 (3.16) years, respectively. Groups were similar in age and education. Eleven one-way ANOVAs were implemented across WAIS-R Similarities and Block Design, Rey Figure Copy, Boston Naming Test (BNT), COWAT, WMS-R Logical Memory (LM) I and II, Verbal Paired Associates (VPA) I and II, and Visual Reproduction (VR) I and II. Results: ANOVAs were significant for Similarities, BNT, LM I and II, VPA I and II, and VR II. LSD post-hoc tests demonstrated that the ADC group did not differ on any measures from the AD group. Similarly, the ADC group generally showed the same relationships to the VaD group as did the AD subjects. Conclusions: The presence of micro- or macro-vessel disease has been tied to cognitive decline in elderly patients. Due to the high incidence of vascular diseases in elderly patients, it has been suggested that cases of pure AD are quite rare, and that dementing disorders are generally of mixed etiology. However, according to the results of the current study, it appears that the impact of cardiac disease in Alzheimer's patients was negligible, as both ADC and AD groups were cognitively similar, and were deficient when compared with the VaD group.
